{"timestamp":"2026-11-02T20:10:06.522Z","ordinal":44824,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a192c0-f277-7f60-8574-21ce71edb8c2","turn_id":"01a19de1-2f43-7663-a2d9-f17adf8cb101","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-2c5d0b42-39f8-4427-bbcb-015358f9524e","process_id":"90808","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","printf '%s\\n' '--- v1.1.2 ROADMAP (origin/main) ---'; git show origin/main:docs/v1.1.2/ROADMAP.md | sed -n '1,240p'; printf '%s\\n' '--- v1.1.2 PLAN (origin/main) ---'; git show origin/main:docs/v1.1.2/PLAN.md | sed -n '1,220p'; printf '%s\\n' '--- origin deletion paths ---'; git show --first-parent --name-status --format='%H %s' a0d374e8 | rg '^(commit|D\\s+|A\\s+|M\\s+)(cli/test/eval_dataset|docs/releases/v1.1.2|docs/v1.1.0/WP-1103|docs/v1.1.2)'"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-wt/v112-wp1104-integration","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"printf '%s\\n' '--- v1.1.2 ROADMAP (origin/main) ---'; git show origin/main:docs/v1.1.2/ROADMAP.md | sed -n '1,240p'; printf '%s\\n' '--- v1.1.2 PLAN (origin/main) ---'; git show origin/main:docs/v1.1.2/PLAN.md | sed -n '1,220p'; printf '%s\\n' '--- origin deletion paths ---'; git show --first-parent --name-status --format='%H %s' a0d374e8 | rg '^(commit|D\\s+|A\\s+|M\\s+)(cli/test/eval_dataset|docs/releases/v1.1.2|docs/v1.1.0/WP-1103|docs/v1.1.2)'"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"--- v1.1.2 ROADMAP (origin/main) ---\n# Nexus Code v1.1.2 Roadmap: Specialist Orchestration & Independent Review\n\n**Milestone:** Nexus Code `v1.1.2`  \n**Status:** In Progress  \n**Owner:** Principal Software Architect & Senior Implementation Engineer  \n**Document ID:** `NEXUS-ROADMAP-V112`  \n**Base Release:** `v1.1.1` (`b9dd0bcc`) / `v1.1.2-rc.1` (`c3e26103`)  \n\n---\n\n## 1. Roadmap Overview & Commitments\n\nThe v1.1.2 release line encompasses two complementary tiers:\n1. **Tier 1 (Completed in v1.1.2-rc.1):** Operational defect fix for multi-message prompt deduplication upon crash-resume ([PR #377](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/377)) and release documentation addendum.\n2. **Tier 2 (v1.1.2 Feature Enhancement):** Bounded specialist agent contracts, objective independent review boundaries (eliminating implementer confirmation bias), deterministic micro-routing (cut from PR #396), and telemetry-aware scheduling.\n\n### Status in PR #396 (owner decision: connect the new modules or cut them)\n- **Item 1, connected.** The specialist contracts are runtime roles in `cli/src/core/roles.ts`. An operation unit or `/team` target can run as one. The agent offers only the contract's permitted tools, and it refuses any other call when the call is dispatched (`roleToolAllowList`). That holds whether or not the role is read-only.\n- **Item 2, connected.** Units with the `reviewer` or `acceptance_reviewer` role run behind the boundary:\n  - Their prompt is the evidence package: the candidate diff against the operation's base commit, and the verifier receipt of the unit they depend on. Prerequisite summaries are withheld.\n  - The unit completes only on a well-formed `ACCEPT` that survives the deterministic gates. Prose, a malformed verdict, a reported defect or a failed verifier blocks the unit for an operator decision.\n  - The verifier receipt comes from the dependency's integration receipt (isolated operations) or, where nothing was integrated (for example `/team`), from the verification recorded on the dependency's completed result. A verification that is not a passing run that executed tests reaches the package as a non-zero exit code. A large (truncated) diff with no passing verifier evidence is rejected.\n  - Retrying a rejected review resumes the reviewer's conversation. A review unit or read-only specialist (`reviewer`, `acceptance_reviewer`, `semantic_explorer`, `call_site_analyzer`, `security_reviewer`; read-only by construction) does not inherit the write-verification obligation that recovery adds for writer units, since it can neither have written nor run a verifier; writer units, `failure_debugger` (which can run commands) and the read-only `researcher` keep the fail-closed obligation.\n  - The base a review diffs against is the isolated operation's base commit or, for a non-isolated operation in a Git checkout, the HEAD recorded when the operation first ran (`startCommit`), so a change a unit committed is still in the diff. The base is recorded only before any unit has started; an operation persisted without one, whose work already ran, is never given a base afterwards, and its review blocks. A persisted base is resolved with `git rev-parse --verify --end-of-options` before use.\n  - `security_reviewer` is deliberately not behind the boundary: only `reviewer` and `acceptance_reviewer` are. It is a read-only specialist whose contract lists `unified_diff` as an input, and it keeps the ordinary prompt.\n  - An empty candidate diff is treated as a failure to collect evidence (there is nothing to judge), and the worst verification across all dependencies decides the verifier exit code.\n  - If the candidate diff cannot be collected (no Git checkout, or the diff exceeds the 4 MiB buffer), the review is blocked before any model turn. That failure is never presented to the reviewer as evidence.\n  - The runtime package names the real operation id and declares no file scope: no operation or unit declares one, so the scope gate (`detectScopeViolations`) applies only to a package that supplies `authorizedScope`.\n  - The boundary complements `cli/src/core/independent_check.ts`, which decides whether a verifier pass is independent evidence. It does not replace it.\n- **Items 3 and 4, cut.** Nothing in the runtime routed on the micro-router. The escalation machine duplicated the non-progress recovery in PR #398, which is connected.\n- **`TokenBudgetTracker`, cut.** It duplicated `UsageLedger` (`cli/src/usage.ts`), which already enforces `NEXUS_CLOUD_MAX_TOKENS` with in-flight reservations.\n- **Integrated with PR #398.** The recovery context's \"Permitted next actions\" lists only actions the agent's role and mode allow (`renderRecoveryContext`'s `permits` predicate).\n- **Item 5 and the CERI and comparative harness entry points are not yet connected.** The throttle needs request latency from `cli/src/client.ts`. CERI needs a `nexus eval` command in `cli/src/index.ts`. Both files are claimed by other open work.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Workstream Roadmap Specifications\n\n### Item 1: Bounded Specialist Agent Profiles (Workstream A)\n- **Objective:** Introduce typed specialist profiles with strict tool permissions, input/output contracts, and token budget bounds.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** `ROLE_PROFILES` has 5 untyped text roles. No structured JSON schemas, no granular tool whitelisting beyond binary read-only mode.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Create `cli/src/core/specialist_profiles.ts`.\n  - Introduce `SpecialistContract`: permitted tools, input requirements, structured output schema, max context tokens, and escalation thresholds.\n  - Add specialist roles: `semantic_explorer`, `call_site_analyzer`, `failure_debugger`, `security_reviewer`, `acceptance_reviewer`.\n- **Dependencies:** `cli/src/core/roles.ts`, `cli/src/core/tool_groups.ts`.\n- **Priority:** High (P1) — foundational.\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Specialist units reject tools not in their declared profile with actionable errors.\n  - Specialist units emit structured, validated output artifacts.\n- **Verification Method:** Unit tests in `cli/test/specialist_profiles.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Purely additive; existing 5 roles remain backward-compatible defaults.\n- **Complexity:** Medium (3 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 2: Objective Independent Review Boundary (Workstream B)\n- **Objective:** Eliminate reviewer confirmation bias by constructing an objective evidence package stripped of the implementer's narrative claims, enforcing structured review findings.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** Reviewer units currently receive the implementer's summary narrative (`Completed prerequisite work: - <title>: <summary>`), introducing confirmation bias.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Create `cli/src/core/independent_review.ts`.\n  - Implement `buildReviewEvidencePackage`: extracts objective task requirements, authorized scope, base commit, unified diff, raw verifier receipts, and deliberately omits implementer turn narratives.\n  - Enforce structured review verdict schema:\n    ```ts\n    interface IndependentReviewVerdict {\n      verdict: \"ACCEPT\" | \"REJECT\";\n      verifiedObservations: string[];\n      suspectedDefects: Array<{ path: string; line?: number; description: string }>;\n      unprovenCriteria: string[];\n      scopeViolations: string[];\n    }\n    ```\n  - Disallow model-generated metrics from establishing authoritative `VERIFIED` status without deterministic verifier evidence.\n- **Dependencies:** Item 1 (`SpecialistContract`), `cli/src/core/independent_check.ts`.\n- **Priority:** Critical (P0) — addresses core verification integrity.\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Reviewer prompt contains zero sentences authored by the implementer.\n  - Reviewer output parse failure fails-closed as `REJECT`.\n  - Scope violations in diff are flagged deterministically.\n- **Verification Method:** Integration tests in `cli/test/independent_review.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Guarded by operation policy flag `policy.enforceIndependentReviewBoundary`.\n- **Complexity:** Medium (3 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 3: Deterministic Micro-Routing Engine (Workstream C) — cut from PR #396\n- **Objective:** Eliminate unnecessary LLM turns for non-semantic, deterministic commands and routine file/test operations.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** All in-turn tool calls are routed through the LLM. No classification for deterministic fast-paths.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Create `cli/src/core/micro_router.ts`.\n  - Implement rule-based intent matching for pure queries (test execution, symbol search, file reading).\n  - Constrain model-assisted routing with fallback to deterministic defaults.\n- **Dependencies:** None.\n- **Priority:** Medium (P2).\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Deterministic queries bypass inference turns with zero latency regression.\n  - Ambiguous queries fall back gracefully to model classification with full provenance logging.\n- **Verification Method:** Unit tests in `cli/test/micro_router.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Configurable via `policy.deterministicRouting: boolean`.\n- **Complexity:** Low-Medium (2 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 4: Multi-Stage Escalation State Machine (Workstream E) — cut from PR #396; superseded by PR #398 non-progress recovery\n- **Objective:** Replace naive retry loops with an observable escalation state machine that detects repeat failures, engages failure investigators, and escalates to deep reasoning models before blocking for operator input.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** Units retry naively up to `maxRunsPerUnit` on the same resource without diagnostic enrichment.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - State transitions: `INITIAL_ATTEMPT` -> `DIAGNOSTIC_ANALYSIS` -> `DEEP_REASONING_ESCALATION` -> `OPERATOR_DECISION`.\n  - Loop detection: identical error signatures trigger immediate escalation rather than burning budget.\n- **Dependencies:** Item 1 (Specialist Profiles).\n- **Priority:** Medium (P2).\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Repeated identical test failures trigger diagnostic specialist after 1 retry.\n  - Escalation respects model capability requirements and token bounds.\n- **Verification Method:** Unit tests in `cli/test/escalation_machine.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Additive state metadata; backward compatible with v2 schema.\n- **Complexity:** Medium (3 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 5: Telemetry-Informed Concurrency Throttle (Workstream F)\n- **Objective:** Dynamically adjust concurrent execution slots based on worker queue wait, TTFT, and latency telemetry.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** Static integer caps (`policy.maxConcurrentUnits`, `resource.maxConcurrentUnits`).\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Extend `ResourceRegistry` with rolling latency tracking.\n  - Throttle concurrency when latency spikes > 2x baseline, recovering on normalization.\n- **Dependencies:** `cli/src/core/resources.ts`.\n- **Priority:** Low-Medium (P3).\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Worker overload dynamically reduces active concurrency to prevent timeout cascades.\n- **Verification Method:** Simulation tests in `cli/test/adaptive_concurrency.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Can be disabled via `policy.adaptiveThrottle: false`.\n- **Complexity:** Medium (3 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 6: Comparative Evaluation Benchmark Harness (Workstream G)\n- **Objective:** Measure and contrast Configuration A (Baseline) vs Configuration B (Specialist + Independent Review) vs Configuration C (Adaptive).\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** Individual tests exist, but no unified comparative benchmark measuring token efficiency, defect escape rate, and confirmation bias.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Create `eval/comparative_orchestration_harness.ts` / `cli/src/eval/comparative_harness.ts`.\n  - Execute standard suites with telemetry recording.\n- **Dependencies:** Items 1, 2, 3.\n- **Priority:** Medium (P2).\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Produces structured comparative CSV/JSON metrics across identical benchmark tasks.\n- **Verification Method:** Benchmark dry-run on synthetic tasks.\n- **Complexity:** Medium (4 days).\n- **Implementation Status:** Complete in `cli/src/eval/comparative_harness.ts` (Commit `ad8ea909`).\n\n---\n\n### Item 7: Continuous Evaluation and Regression Intelligence (CERI)\n- **Objective:** Establish a continuous, evidence-based mechanism for detecting, investigating, classifying, and reporting degradation in Nexus's end-to-end engineering capabilities, enforcing that internal evaluations may veto releases but never certify correctness without independent validation.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** `eval/run.mjs` and `cli/src/evaluator.ts` execute tasks and capture candidate receipts, but lack versioned evaluation definitions, run manifests with full model/hardware provenance, designated baseline comparison, 12-state comparative classification, regression intelligence with epistemological distinction, and non-self-approving remediation pathways.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Workstream A: Versioned evaluation definitions and run manifests capturing immutable commit, dirty status, model identity, toolchain, and host environment.\n  - Workstream B: Append-only evidence persistence with atomic writes, secret redaction, and strict separation between tamper-evidence and independent validation receipts.\n  - Workstream C: Baseline comparator with eligibility validation and 12-state classification (`PASS`, `FAIL`, `REGRESSION_CANDIDATE`, `CONFIRMED_REGRESSION`, `IMPROVEMENT_CANDIDATE`, `FLAKY`, `INFRASTRUCTURE_FAILURE`, `EVALUATOR_DEFECT`, `INCONCLUSIVE`, `NOT_EXECUTED`, `CANCELLED`, `INCOMPARABLE`).\n  - Workstream D: Repeatability and flakiness analysis with bounded rerun policies.\n  - Workstream E: Capacity-aware scheduling for local dual-worker Intel Arc Pro environments.\n  - Workstream F: Representative end-to-end engineering evaluation benchmarks.\n  - Workstream G: Regression intelligence structuring findings into observation, inference, hypothesis, confirmed finding, and unresolved uncertainty.\n  - Workstream H: Reviewable remediation proposals with non-self-approval boundary.\n  - Workstream I: Structured machine-readable operational reporting with measurement limitations and blockers.\n- **Dependencies:** Items 1-6, `CandidateTracker`, `ValidationReceipt`, `isolate.mjs`.\n- **Priority:** High (P1) — Release qualification gate.\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Full provenance captured in run manifests without fabricating missing metadata.\n  - Incomparable baselines rejected explicitly rather than silently compared.\n  - Binary pass/fail forcing eliminated in favor of 12-state classification.\n  - Internal evaluations cannot mark candidates as verified without deterministic verifier receipts.\n  - Remediation proposals enforce `approvalStatus: \"PENDING_REVIEW\"`.\n- **Verification Method:** Unit and integration test suites in `cli/test/ceri_*.test.ts`.\n- **Operational Risks:** False regression alarms on non-comparable environments; benchmark overfitting; token consumption during reruns.\n- **Rollback Approach:** Purely additive modules in `cli/src/ceri/`; gated by config flag `ceri.enabled`.\n- **Complexity:** High (5 days).\n- **Implementation Status:** Foundational primitives and libraries implemented in PR #396 as standalone modules, covered by unit tests only and not yet used by the agent runtime (contracts, tamper-evident evidence store, baseline comparator, epistemological investigation, non-self-approving remediation, operational reporting, and comparative evaluation framework). Interactive agent loop integration and live dual-worker GPU scheduling are scheduled for subsequent integration PRs.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Deferred Scope (Targeted for v1.2)\n\nThe following items are explicitly **deferred** to release v1.2:\n1. **Hosted / Multi-Tenant Concurrency:** Nexus trust boundary remains local single-operator.\n2. **Autonomous Tool Generation:** Dynamic creation of new tool definitions by models without human oversight.\n3. **Automatic PR Publication (`git.publish`):** Requires operation-level egress and policy controls.\n4. **Interactive TUI Multi-Agent Pane:** Requires terminal PTY driver redesign.\n--- v1.1.2 PLAN (origin/main) ---\n# Nexus Code v1.1.2 Implementation Plan: Specialist Orchestration & Independent Review\n\n**Document ID:** `NEXUS-PLAN-V112`  \n**Author:** Principal Software Architect & Senior Implementation Engineer  \n**Status:** Approved for Implementation  \n**Base Revision:** `origin/main` (`9a3d8787`)  \n\n---\n\n## 1. Architectural Strategy & Phasing\n\nTo maintain production stability and backward compatibility, the implementation proceeds in five ordered vertical slices:\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Slice 1: Specialist Agent Contracts & Profiles (Foundation) │\n│ - Typed SpecialistContract schema                           │\n│ - Tool permission whitelists per specialist                 │\n│ - Structured output envelope                                │\n└──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘\n                               │\n┌──────────────────────────────▼──────────────────────────────┐\n│ Slice 2: Objective Independent Review Boundary (Integrity)  │\n│ - buildReviewEvidencePackage (strips narrative)             │\n│ - Strict IndependentReviewVerdict schema                    │\n│ - Verification gating rules                                 │\n└──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘\n                               │\n┌──────────────────────────────▼──────────────────────────────┐\n│ Slice 3: Deterministic Micro-Routing Engine (Efficiency)    │\n│ - Rule-based fast-path matching for non-semantic queries    │\n│ - Constrained model-assisted routing fallback               │\n└──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘\n                               │\n┌──────────────────────────────▼──────────────────────────────┐\n│ Slice 4: Multi-Stage Escalation State Machine (Resilience)  │\n│ - Repeat failure detection & diagnostic routing             │\n│ - Deep reasoning escalation path (Qwen3.5-27B)              │\n└──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘\n                               │\n┌──────────────────────────────▼──────────────────────────────┐\n│ Slice 5: Telemetry-Informed Concurrency Throttle & Harness  │\n│ - Dynamic slot throttle under worker latency pressure       │\n│ - Comparative benchmark harness                             │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Slice 1: Specialist Agent Contracts & Profiles\n\n### 2.1 Modules & Files Changed\n- **New Module:** `cli/src/core/specialist_profiles.ts`\n- **Updated Module:** `cli/src/core/roles.ts` (exporting expanded roles and contracts)\n- **New Tests:** `cli/test/specialist_profiles.test.ts`\n\n### 2.2 Data Contracts & Interfaces\n```ts\nexport type SpecialistRole =\n  | \"semantic_explorer\"\n  | \"call_site_analyzer\"\n  | \"failure_debugger\"\n  | \"security_reviewer\"\n  | \"acceptance_reviewer\";\n\nexport interface SpecialistContract {\n  role: SpecialistRole;\n  title: string;\n  description: string;\n  permittedTools: readonly string[];\n  readOnly: boolean;\n  maxContextTokens: number;\n  inputRequirements: readonly string[];\n  outputSchemaName: string;\n}\n\nexport interface SpecialistStructuredOutput<T = unknown> {\n  specialistRole: SpecialistRole;\n  status: \"SUCCESS\" | \"FAILED\" | \"INCONCLUSIVE\";\n  findings: T;\n  evidence: Array<{ path: string; lineRange?: [number, number]; note: string }>;\n  tokenConsumption?: { prompt: number; completion: number };\n}\n```\n\n### 2.3 Invariants & Security\n- A specialist assigned to a unit cannot execute tools outside `permittedTools`. If a prohibited tool is called, the execution harness fails-closed with code `E_TOOL_DISALLOWED`.\n- Read-only specialists (`readOnly: true`) strictly enforce `--read-only` flag on the runner.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Slice 2: Objective Independent Review Boundary\n\n### 3.1 Modules & Files Changed\n- **New Module:** `cli/src/core/independent_review.ts`\n- **Updated Module:** `cli/src/core/operation_runner_execution.ts` (using objective evidence package when building reviewer prompts)\n- **New Tests:** `cli/test/independent_review.test.ts`\n\n### 3.2 Data Contracts & Interfaces\n```ts\nexport interface ReviewEvidencePackage {\n  operationId: string;\n  unitId: string;\n  objective: string;\n  authorizedScope: readonly string[];\n  baselineCommit: string;\n  candidateCommit?: string;\n  unifiedDiff: string;\n  verifierOutput?: string;\n  testPassRate?: { passed: number; failed: number; total: number };\n}\n\nexport interface IndependentReviewVerdict {\n  verdict: \"ACCEPT\" | \"REJECT\";\n  verifiedObservations: string[];\n  suspectedDefects: Array<{ path: string; line?: number; description: string }>;\n  unprovenCriteria: string[];\n  scopeViolations: string[];\n}\n```\n\n### 3.3 Confirmation Bias Mitigation\n- `buildReviewEvidencePackage`:\n  - Strips all free-form text narratives authored by the implementer.\n  - Injects only: (1) Objective requirements, (2) authorized file paths, (3) Git unified diff, (4) raw compiler/test runner stdout/stderr.\n  - Reviewer prompt states: `\"You are evaluating the raw code changes and test evidence independently. The implementer's self-assessed completion notes have been intentionally redacted.\"`\n\n---\n\n## 4. Slice 3: Deterministic Micro-Routing Engine (cut from PR #396)\n\n> Not delivered: nothing in the runtime routed on it, so `micro_router.ts` and its tests were removed. The design below is kept as history. See `ROADMAP.md`, \"Status in PR #396\".\n\n### 4.1 Modules & Files Changed\n- **New Module:** `cli/src/core/micro_router.ts`\n- **New Tests:** `cli/test/micro_router.test.ts`\n\n### 4.2 Interfaces\n```ts\nexport type RoutingDecisionKind = \"DETERMINISTIC_FASTPATH\" | \"SPECIALIST_DELEGATION\" | \"GENERAL_MODEL\";\n\nexport interface RoutingDecision {\n  kind: RoutingDecisionKind;\n  matchedRule?: string;\n  targetTool?: string;\n  targetRole?: string;\n  reason: string;\n  confidence: number;\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Persistence, State, & Migration Implications\n\n- **Schema Stability:** The operation persistence schema (`cli/src/core/operation.ts`) remains on **`schemaVersion: 2`**.\n- Specialist contracts and review packages operate cleanly within existing `OperationMessage` (kinds `\"artifact\"` and `\"verdict\"`) and `WorkUnit` models.\n- No schema migration required from v1.1.1 or v1.1.2-rc.1.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Verification Milestones\n\n1. **Milestone 1 (Specialist Profiles):** `specialist_profiles.test.ts` passes with 100% test coverage; tool whitelist rejections validated.\n2. **Milestone 2 (Review Boundary):** `independent_review.test.ts` passes; implementer narrative redaction verified; structured verdict parsing verified.\n3. **Milestone 3 (Micro-Router, cut from PR #396):** `micro_router.test.ts` passes; rule-based queries execute without LLM round-trips.\n4. **Milestone 4 (Full Regression):** Complete existing test suite (`npm --prefix cli test`) runs without regressions.\n--- origin deletion paths ---\nA\tdocs/v1.1.2/ARCHITECTURE_ASSESSMENT.md\nA\tdocs/v1.1.2/CERI_ARCHITECTURE_ASSESSMENT.md\nA\tdocs/v1.1.2/CERI_PLAN.md\nA\tdocs/v1.1.2/PLAN.md\nA\tdocs/v1.1.2/ROADMAP.md\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":804107319}},"started_at_ms":1793650205716,"completed_at_ms":1793650206521}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-02T20:10:07.990Z","ordinal":44825,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a19de2-2cf6-71a2-8136-f21836150bd6","call_id":"call_1c2290da6ef749a9bc78e5ad43327aa1","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 4.0 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 6118)\nTotal output lines: 363\n\n--- v1.1.2 ROADMAP (origin/main) ---\n# Nexus Code v1.1.2 Roadmap: Specialist Orchestration & Independent Review\n\n**Milestone:** Nexus Code `v1.1.2`  \n**Status:** In Progress  \n**Owner:** Principal Software Architect & Senior Implementation Engineer  \n**Document ID:** `NEXUS-ROADMAP-V112`  \n**Base Release:** `v1.1.1` (`b9dd0bcc`) / `v1.1.2-rc.1` (`c3e26103`)  \n\n---\n\n## 1. Roadmap Overview & Commitments\n\nThe v1.1.2 release line encompasses two complementary tiers:\n1. **Tier 1 (Completed in v1.1.2-rc.1):** Operational defect fix for multi-message prompt deduplication upon crash-resume ([PR #377](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/377)) and release documentation addendum.\n2. **Tier 2 (v1.1.2 Feature Enhancement):** Bounded specialist agent contracts, objective independent review boundaries (eliminating implementer confirmation bias), deterministic micro-routing (cut from PR #396), and telemetry-aware scheduling.\n\n### Status in PR #396 (owner decision: connect the new modules or cut them)\n- **Item 1, connected.** The specialist contracts are runtime roles in `cli/src/core/roles.ts`. An operation unit or `/team` target can run as one. The agent offers only the contract's permitted tools, and it refuses any other call when the call is dispatched (`roleToolAllowList`). That holds whether or not the role is read-only.\n- **Item 2, connected.** Units with the `reviewer` or `acceptance_reviewer` role run behind the boundary:\n  - Their prompt is the evidence package: the candidate diff against the operation's base commit, and the verifier receipt of the unit they depend on. Prerequisite summaries are withheld.\n  - The unit completes only on a well-formed `ACCEPT` that survives the deterministic gates. Prose, a malformed verdict, a reported defect or a failed verifier blocks the unit for an operator decision.\n  - The verifier receipt comes from the dependency's integration receipt (isolated operations) or, where nothing was integrated (for example `/team`), from the verification recorded on the dependency's completed result. A verification that is not a passing run that executed tests reaches the package as a non-zero exit code. A large (truncated) diff with no passing verifier evidence is rejected.\n  - Retrying a rejected review resumes the reviewer's conversation. A review unit or read-only specialist (`reviewer`, `acceptance_reviewer`, `semantic_explorer`, `call_site_analyzer`, `security_reviewer`; read-only by construction) does not inherit the write-verification obligation that recovery adds for writer units, since it can neither have written nor run a verifier; writer units, `failure_debugger` (which can run commands) and the read-only `researcher` keep the fail-closed obligation.\n  - The base a review diffs against is the isolated operation's base commit or, for a non-isolated operation in a Git checkout, the HEAD recorded when the operation first ran (`startCommit`), so a change a unit committed is still in the diff. The base is recorded only before any unit has started; an operation persisted without one, whose work already ran, is never given a base afterwards, and its review blocks. A persisted base is resolved with `git rev-parse --verify --end-of-options` before use.\n  - `security_reviewer` is deliberately not behind the boundary: only `reviewer` and `acceptance_reviewer` are. It is a read-only specialist whose contract lists `unified_diff` as an input, and it keeps the ordinary prompt.\n  - An empty candidate diff is treated as a failure to collect evidence (there is nothing to judge), and the worst verification across all dependencies decides the verifier exit code.\n  - If the candidate diff cannot be collected (no Git checkout, or the diff exceeds the 4 MiB buffer), the review is blocked before any model turn. That failure is never presented to the reviewer as evidence.\n  - The runtime package names the real operation id and declares no file scope: no operation or unit declares one, so the scope gate (`detectScopeViolations`) applies only to a package that supplies `authorizedScope`.\n  - The boundary complements `cli/src/core/independent_check.ts`, which decides whether a verifier pass is independent evidence. It does not replace it.\n- **Items 3 and 4, cut.** Nothing in the runtime routed on the micro-router. The escalation machine duplicated the non-progress recovery in PR #398, which is connected.\n- **`TokenBudgetTracker`, cut.** It duplicated `UsageLedger` (`cli/src/usage.ts`), which already enforces `NEXUS_CLOUD_MAX_TOKENS` with in-flight reservations.\n- **Integrated with PR #398.** The recovery context's \"Permitted next actions\" lists only actions the agent's role and mode allow (`renderRecoveryContext`'s `permits` predicate).\n- **Item 5 and the CERI and comparative harness entry points are not yet connected.** The throttle needs request latency from `cli/src/client.ts`. CERI needs a `nexus eval` command in `cli/src/index.ts`. Both files are claimed by other open work.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Workstream Roadmap Specifications\n\n### Item 1: Bounded Specialist Agent Profiles (Workstream A)\n- **Objective:** Introduce typed specialist profiles with strict tool permissions, input/output contracts, and token budget bounds.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** `ROLE_PROFILES` has 5 untyped text roles. No structured JSON schemas, no granular tool whitelisting beyond binary read-only mode.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Create `cli/src/core/specialist_profiles.ts`.\n  - Introduce `SpecialistContract`: permitted tools, input requirements, structured output schema, max context tokens, and escalation thresholds.\n  - Add specialist roles: `semantic_explorer`, `call_site_analyzer`, `failure_debugger`, `security_reviewer`, `acceptance_reviewer`.\n- **Dependencies:** `cli/src/core/roles.ts`, `cli/src/core/tool_groups.ts`.\n- **Priority:** High (P1) — foundational.\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Specialist units reject tools not in their declared profile with actionable errors.\n  - Specialist units emit structured, validated output artifacts.\n- **Verification Method:** Unit tests in `cli/test/specialist_profiles.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Purely additive; existing 5 roles remain backward-compatible defaults.\n- **Complexity:** Medium (3 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 2: Objective Independent Review Boundary (Workstream B)\n- **Objective:** Eliminate reviewer confirmation bias by constructing an objective evidence package stripped of the implementer's narrative claims, enforcing structured review findings.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** Reviewer units currently receive the implementer's summary narrative (`Completed prerequisite work: - <title>: <summary>`), introducing confirmation bias.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Create `cli/src/core/independent_review.ts`.\n  - Implement `buildReviewEvidencePackage`: extracts objective task requirements, authorized scope, base commit, unified diff, raw verifier receipts, and deliberately omits implementer turn narratives.\n  - Enforce structured review verdict schema:\n    ```ts\n    interface IndependentReviewVerdict {\n      verdict: \"ACCEPT\" | \"REJECT\";\n      verifiedObservations: string[];\n      suspectedDefects: Array<{ path: string; line?: number; description: string }>;\n      unprovenCriteria: string[];\n      scopeViolations: string[];\n    }\n    ```\n  - Disallow model-generated metrics from establishing authoritative `VERIFIED` status without deterministic verifier evidence.\n- **Dependencies:** Item 1 (`SpecialistContract`), `cli/src/core/independent_check.ts`.\n- **Priority:** Critical (P0) — addresses core verification integrity.\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Reviewer prompt contains zero sentences authored by the implementer.\n  - Reviewer output parse failure fails-closed as `REJECT`.\n  - Scope violations in diff are flagged deterministically.\n- **Verification Method:** Integration tests in `cli/test/independent_review.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Guarded by operation policy flag `policy.enforceIndependentReviewBoundary`.\n- **Complexity:** Medium (3 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 3: Deterministic Micro-Routing Engine (Workstream C) — cut from PR #396\n- **Objective:** Eliminate unnecessary LLM turns for non-semantic, deterministic commands and routine file/test operations.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** All in-turn tool calls are routed through the LLM. No classification for deterministic fast-paths.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Create `cli/src/core/micro_router.ts`.\n  - Implement rule-based intent matching for pure queries (test execution, symbol search, file reading).\n  - Constrain model-assisted routing with fallback to deterministic defaults.\n- **Dependencies:** None.\n- **Priority:** Medium (P2).\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Deterministic queries bypass inference turns with zero latency regression.\n  - Ambiguous queries fall back gracefully to model classification with full provenance logging.\n- **Verification Method:** Unit tests in `cli/test/micro_router.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Configurable via `policy.deterministicRouting: boolean`.\n- **Complexity:** Low-Medium (2 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 4: Multi-Stage Escalation State Machine (Workstream E) — cut from PR #396; superseded by PR #398 non-progress recovery\n- **Objective:** Replace naive retry loops with an observable escalation state machine that detects repeat failures, engages failure investigators, and escalates to deep reasoning models before blocking for operator input.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** Units retry naively up to `maxRunsPerUnit` on the same resource without diagnostic enrichment.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - State transitions: `INITIAL_ATTEMPT` -> `DIAGNOSTIC_ANALYSIS` -> `DEEP_REASONING_ESCALATION` -> `OPERATOR_DECISION`.\n  - Loop detection: identical error signatures trigger immediate escalation rather than burning budget.\n- **Dependencies:** Item 1 (Specialist Profiles).\n- **Priority:** Medium (P2).\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Repeated identical test failures trigger diagnostic specialist after 1 retry.\n  - Escalation respects model capability requirements and token bounds.\n- **Verification Method:** Unit tests in `cli/test/escalation_machine.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Additive state metadata; backward compatible with v2 schema.\n- **Complexity:** Medium (3 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 5: Telemetry-Informed Concurrency Throttle (Workstream F)\n- **Objective:** Dynamically adjust concurrent execution slots based on worker queue wait, TTFT, and latency telemetry.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** Static integer caps (`policy.maxConcurrentUnits`, `resource.maxConcurrentUnits`).\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Extend `ResourceRegistry` with rolling latency tracking.\n  - Throttle concurrency when latency spikes > 2x baseline, recovering on normalization.\n- **Dependencies:** `cli/src/core/resources.ts`.\n- **Priority:** Low-Medium (P3).\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Worker overload dynamically reduces active concurrency to prevent timeout cascades.\n- **Verification Method:** Simulation tests in `cli/test/adaptive_concurrency.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Can be disabled via `policy.adaptiveThrottle: false`.\n- **Complexity:** Medium (3 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 6: Comparative Evaluation Benchmark Harness (Workstream G)\n- **Objective:** Measure and contrast Configuration A (Baseline) vs Configuration B (Specialist + Independent Review) vs Configuration C (Adaptive).\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** Individual tests exist, but no unified comparative benchmark measuring token efficiency, defect escape rate, and confirmation bias.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Create `eval/comparative_orchestration_harness.ts` / `cli/src/eval/comparative_harness.ts`.\n  - Execute standard suites with telemetry recording.\n- **Dependencies:** Items 1, 2, 3.\n- **Priority:** Medium (P2).\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Produces structured comparative CSV/JSON metrics across identical benchmark tasks.\n- **Verification Method:** B…118 tokens truncated… veto releases but never certify correctness without independent validation.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** `eval/run.mjs` and `cli/src/evaluator.ts` execute tasks and capture candidate receipts, but lack versioned evaluation definitions, run manifests with full model/hardware provenance, designated baseline comparison, 12-state comparative classification, regression intelligence with epistemological distinction, and non-self-approving remediation pathways.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Workstream A: Versioned evaluation definitions and run manifests capturing immutable commit, dirty status, model identity, toolchain, and host environment.\n  - Workstream B: Append-only evidence persistence with atomic writes, secret redaction, and strict separation between tamper-evidence and independent validation receipts.\n  - Workstream C: Baseline comparator with eligibility validation and 12-state classification (`PASS`, `FAIL`, `REGRESSION_CANDIDATE`, `CONFIRMED_REGRESSION`, `IMPROVEMENT_CANDIDATE`, `FLAKY`, `INFRASTRUCTURE_FAILURE`, `EVALUATOR_DEFECT`, `INCONCLUSIVE`, `NOT_EXECUTED`, `CANCELLED`, `INCOMPARABLE`).\n  - Workstream D: Repeatability and flakiness analysis with bounded rerun policies.\n  - Workstream E: Capacity-aware scheduling for local dual-worker Intel Arc Pro environments.\n  - Workstream F: Representative end-to-end engineering evaluation benchmarks.\n  - Workstream G: Regression intelligence structuring findings into observation, inference, hypothesis, confirmed finding, and unresolved uncertainty.\n  - Workstream H: Reviewable remediation proposals with non-self-approval boundary.\n  - Workstream I: Structured machine-readable operational reporting with measurement limitations and blockers.\n- **Dependencies:** Items 1-6, `CandidateTracker`, `ValidationReceipt`, `isolate.mjs`.\n- **Priority:** High (P1) — Release qualification gate.\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Full provenance captured in run manifests without fabricating missing metadata.\n  - Incomparable baselines rejected explicitly rather than silently compared.\n  - Binary pass/fail forcing eliminated in favor of 12-state classification.\n  - Internal evaluations cannot mark candidates as verified without deterministic verifier receipts.\n  - Remediation proposals enforce `approvalStatus: \"PENDING_REVIEW\"`.\n- **Verification Method:** Unit and integration test suites in `cli/test/ceri_*.test.ts`.\n- **Operational Risks:** False regression alarms on non-comparable environments; benchmark overfitting; token consumption during reruns.\n- **Rollback Approach:** Purely additive modules in `cli/src/ceri/`; gated by config flag `ceri.enabled`.\n- **Complexity:** High (5 days).\n- **Implementation Status:** Foundational primitives and libraries implemented in PR #396 as standalone modules, covered by unit tests only and not yet used by the agent runtime (contracts, tamper-evident evidence store, baseline comparator, epistemological investigation, non-self-approving remediation, operational reporting, and comparative evaluation framework). Interactive agent loop integration and live dual-worker GPU scheduling are scheduled for subsequent integration PRs.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Deferred Scope (Targeted for v1.2)\n\nThe following items are explicitly **deferred** to release v1.2:\n1. **Hosted / Multi-Tenant Concurrency:** Nexus trust boundary remains local single-operator.\n2. **Autonomous Tool Generation:** Dynamic creation of new tool definitions by models without human oversight.\n3. **Automatic PR Publication (`git.publish`):** Requires operation-level egress and policy controls.\n4. **Interactive TUI Multi-Agent Pane:** Requires terminal PTY driver redesign.\n--- v1.1.2 PLAN (origin/main) ---\n# Nexus Code v1.1.2 Implementation Plan: Specialist Orchestration & Independent Review\n\n**Document ID:** `NEXUS-PLAN-V112`  \n**Author:** Principal Software Architect & Senior Implementation Engineer  \n**Status:** Approved for Implementation  \n**Base Revision:** `origin/main` (`9a3d8787`)  \n\n---\n\n## 1. Architectural Strategy & Phasing\n\nTo maintain production stability and backward compatibility, the implementation proceeds in five ordered vertical slices:\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Slice 1: Specialist Agent Contracts & Profiles (Foundation) │\n│ - Typed SpecialistContract schema                           │\n│ - Tool permission whitelists per specialist                 │\n│ - Structured output envelope                                │\n└──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘\n                               │\n┌──────────────────────────────▼──────────────────────────────┐\n│ Slice 2: Objective Independent Review Boundary (Integrity)  │\n│ - buildReviewEvidencePackage (strips narrative)             │\n│ - Strict IndependentReviewVerdict schema                    │\n│ - Verification gating rules                                 │\n└──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘\n                               │\n┌──────────────────────────────▼──────────────────────────────┐\n│ Slice 3: Deterministic Micro-Routing Engine (Efficiency)    │\n│ - Rule-based fast-path matching for non-semantic queries    │\n│ - Constrained model-assisted routing fallback               │\n└──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘\n                               │\n┌──────────────────────────────▼──────────────────────────────┐\n│ Slice 4: Multi-Stage Escalation State Machine (Resilience)  │\n│ - Repeat failure detection & diagnostic routing             │\n│ - Deep reasoning escalation path (Qwen3.5-27B)              │\n└──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘\n                               │\n┌──────────────────────────────▼──────────────────────────────┐\n│ Slice 5: Telemetry-Informed Concurrency Throttle & Harness  │\n│ - Dynamic slot throttle under worker latency pressure       │\n│ - Comparative benchmark harness                             │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Slice 1: Specialist Agent Contracts & Profiles\n\n### 2.1 Modules & Files Changed\n- **New Module:** `cli/src/core/specialist_profiles.ts`\n- **Updated Module:** `cli/src/core/roles.ts` (exporting expanded roles and contracts)\n- **New Tests:** `cli/test/specialist_profiles.test.ts`\n\n### 2.2 Data Contracts & Interfaces\n```ts\nexport type SpecialistRole =\n  | \"semantic_explorer\"\n  | \"call_site_analyzer\"\n  | \"failure_debugger\"\n  | \"security_reviewer\"\n  | \"acceptance_reviewer\";\n\nexport interface SpecialistContract {\n  role: SpecialistRole;\n  title: string;\n  description: string;\n  permittedTools: readonly string[];\n  readOnly: boolean;\n  maxContextTokens: number;\n  inputRequirements: readonly string[];\n  outputSchemaName: string;\n}\n\nexport interface SpecialistStructuredOutput<T = unknown> {\n  specialistRole: SpecialistRole;\n  status: \"SUCCESS\" | \"FAILED\" | \"INCONCLUSIVE\";\n  findings: T;\n  evidence: Array<{ path: string; lineRange?: [number, number]; note: string }>;\n  tokenConsumption?: { prompt: number; completion: number };\n}\n```\n\n### 2.3 Invariants & Security\n- A specialist assigned to a unit cannot execute tools outside `permittedTools`. If a prohibited tool is called, the execution harness fails-closed with code `E_TOOL_DISALLOWED`.\n- Read-only specialists (`readOnly: true`) strictly enforce `--read-only` flag on the runner.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Slice 2: Objective Independent Review Boundary\n\n### 3.1 Modules & Files Changed\n- **New Module:** `cli/src/core/independent_review.ts`\n- **Updated Module:** `cli/src/core/operation_runner_execution.ts` (using objective evidence package when building reviewer prompts)\n- **New Tests:** `cli/test/independent_review.test.ts`\n\n### 3.2 Data Contracts & Interfaces\n```ts\nexport interface ReviewEvidencePackage {\n  operationId: string;\n  unitId: string;\n  objective: string;\n  authorizedScope: readonly string[];\n  baselineCommit: string;\n  candidateCommit?: string;\n  unifiedDiff: string;\n  verifierOutput?: string;\n  testPassRate?: { passed: number; failed: number; total: number };\n}\n\nexport interface IndependentReviewVerdict {\n  verdict: \"ACCEPT\" | \"REJECT\";\n  verifiedObservations: string[];\n  suspectedDefects: Array<{ path: string; line?: number; description: string }>;\n  unprovenCriteria: string[];\n  scopeViolations: string[];\n}\n```\n\n### 3.3 Confirmation Bias Mitigation\n- `buildReviewEvidencePackage`:\n  - Strips all free-form text narratives authored by the implementer.\n  - Injects only: (1) Objective requirements, (2) authorized file paths, (3) Git unified diff, (4) raw compiler/test runner stdout/stderr.\n  - Reviewer prompt states: `\"You are evaluating the raw code changes and test evidence independently. The implementer's self-assessed completion notes have been intentionally redacted.\"`\n\n---\n\n## 4. Slice 3: Deterministic Micro-Routing Engine (cut from PR #396)\n\n> Not delivered: nothing in the runtime routed on it, so `micro_router.ts` and its tests were removed. The design below is kept as history. See `ROADMAP.md`, \"Status in PR #396\".\n\n### 4.1 Modules & Files Changed\n- **New Module:** `cli/src/core/micro_router.ts`\n- **New Tests:** `cli/test/micro_router.test.ts`\n\n### 4.2 Interfaces\n```ts\nexport type RoutingDecisionKind = \"DETERMINISTIC_FASTPATH\" | \"SPECIALIST_DELEGATION\" | \"GENERAL_MODEL\";\n\nexport interface RoutingDecision {\n  kind: RoutingDecisionKind;\n  matchedRule?: string;\n  targetTool?: string;\n  targetRole?: string;\n  reason: string;\n  confidence: number;\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Persistence, State, & Migration Implications\n\n- **Schema Stability:** The operation persistence schema (`cli/src/core/operation.ts`) remains on **`schemaVersion: 2`**.\n- Specialist contracts and review packages operate cleanly within existing `OperationMessage` (kinds `\"artifact\"` and `\"verdict\"`) and `WorkUnit` models.\n- No schema migration required from v1.1.1 or v1.1.2-rc.1.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Verification Milestones\n\n1. **Milestone 1 (Specialist Profiles):** `specialist_profiles.test.ts` passes with 100% test coverage; tool whitelist rejections validated.\n2. **Milestone 2 (Review Boundary):** `independent_review.test.ts` passes; implementer narrative redaction verified; structured verdict parsing verified.\n3. **Milestone 3 (Micro-Router, cut from PR #396):** `micro_router.test.ts` passes; rule-based queries execute without LLM round-trips.\n4. **Milestone 4 (Full Regression):** Complete existing test suite (`npm --prefix cli test`) runs without regressions.\n--- origin deletion paths ---\nA\tdocs/v1.1.2/ARCHITECTURE_ASSESSMENT.md\nA\tdocs/v1.1.2/CERI_ARCHITECTURE_ASSESSMENT.md\nA\tdocs/v1.1.2/CERI_PLAN.md\nA\tdocs/v1.1.2/PLAN.md\nA\tdocs/v1.1.2/ROADMAP.md\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a19de1-2f43-7663-a2d9-f17adf8cb101","create_time":1793650207.990411}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
