{"timestamp":"2026-11-02T21:52:58.455Z","ordinal":47728,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a192c0-f277-7f60-8574-21ce71edb8c2","turn_id":"01a19de1-2f43-7663-a2d9-f17adf8cb101","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-26dbe27b-ffb0-4b3d-9380-e740c1b31eed","process_id":"68992","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","cat docs/v1.1.2/ROADMAP.md; sed -n '1,240p' docs/v1.1.2/PLAN.md; sed -n '35,160p' docs/v1.1.0/ROADMAP.md; sed -n '1,180p' docs/releases/v1.1.2-qualification.md"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-wt/v112-wp1103-a0d374e8","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"cat docs/v1.1.2/ROADMAP.md","name":"ROADMAP.md","path":"docs/v1.1.2/ROADMAP.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,240p' docs/v1.1.2/PLAN.md","name":"PLAN.md","path":"docs/v1.1.2/PLAN.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '35,160p' docs/v1.1.0/ROADMAP.md","name":"ROADMAP.md","path":"docs/v1.1.0/ROADMAP.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,180p' docs/releases/v1.1.2-qualification.md","name":"v1.1.2-qualification.md","path":"docs/releases/v1.1.2-qualification.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"# Nexus Code v1.1.2 Roadmap: Specialist Orchestration & Independent Review\n\n**Milestone:** Nexus Code `v1.1.2`  \n**Status:** In Progress  \n**Owner:** Principal Software Architect & Senior Implementation Engineer  \n**Document ID:** `NEXUS-ROADMAP-V112`  \n**Base Release:** `v1.1.1` (`b9dd0bcc`) / `v1.1.2-rc.1` (`c3e26103`)  \n\n---\n\n## 1. Roadmap Overview & Commitments\n\nThe v1.1.2 release line encompasses two complementary tiers:\n1. **Tier 1 (Completed in v1.1.2-rc.1):** Operational defect fix for multi-message prompt deduplication upon crash-resume ([PR #377](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/377)) and release documentation addendum.\n2. **Tier 2 (v1.1.2 Feature Enhancement):** Bounded specialist agent contracts, objective independent review boundaries (eliminating implementer confirmation bias), deterministic micro-routing (cut from PR #396), and telemetry-aware scheduling.\n\n### Status in PR #396 (owner decision: connect the new modules or cut them)\n- **Item 1, connected.** The specialist contracts are runtime roles in `cli/src/core/roles.ts`. An operation unit or `/team` target can run as one. The agent offers only the contract's permitted tools, and it refuses any other call when the call is dispatched (`roleToolAllowList`). That holds whether or not the role is read-only.\n- **Item 2, connected.** Units with the `reviewer` or `acceptance_reviewer` role run behind the boundary:\n  - Their prompt is the evidence package: the candidate diff against the operation's base commit, and the verifier receipt of the unit they depend on. Prerequisite summaries are withheld.\n  - The unit completes only on a well-formed `ACCEPT` that survives the deterministic gates. Prose, a malformed verdict, a reported defect or a failed verifier blocks the unit for an operator decision.\n  - The verifier receipt comes from the dependency's integration receipt (isolated operations) or, where nothing was integrated (for example `/team`), from the verification recorded on the dependency's completed result. A verification that is not a passing run that executed tests reaches the package as a non-zero exit code. A large (truncated) diff with no passing verifier evidence is rejected.\n  - Retrying a rejected review resumes the reviewer's conversation. A review unit or read-only specialist (`reviewer`, `acceptance_reviewer`, `semantic_explorer`, `call_site_analyzer`, `security_reviewer`; read-only by construction) does not inherit the write-verification obligation that recovery adds for writer units, since it can neither have written nor run a verifier; writer units, `failure_debugger` (which can run commands) and the read-only `researcher` keep the fail-closed obligation.\n  - The base a review diffs against is the isolated operation's base commit or, for a non-isolated operation in a Git checkout, the HEAD recorded when the operation first ran (`startCommit`), so a change a unit committed is still in the diff. The base is recorded only before any unit has started; an operation persisted without one, whose work already ran, is never given a base afterwards, and its review blocks. A persisted base is resolved with `git rev-parse --verify --end-of-options` before use.\n  - `security_reviewer` is deliberately not behind the boundary: only `reviewer` and `acceptance_reviewer` are. It is a read-only specialist whose contract lists `unified_diff` as an input, and it keeps the ordinary prompt.\n  - An empty candidate diff is treated as a failure to collect evidence (there is nothing to judge), and the worst verification across all dependencies decides the verifier exit code.\n  - If the candidate diff cannot be collected (no Git checkout, or the diff exceeds the 4 MiB buffer), the review is blocked before any model turn. That failure is never presented to the reviewer as evidence.\n  - The runtime package names the real operation id and declares no file scope: no operation or unit declares one, so the scope gate (`detectScopeViolations`) applies only to a package that supplies `authorizedScope`.\n  - The boundary complements `cli/src/core/independent_check.ts`, which decides whether a verifier pass is independent evidence. It does not replace it.\n- **Items 3 and 4, cut.** Nothing in the runtime routed on the micro-router. The escalation machine duplicated the non-progress recovery in PR #398, which is connected.\n- **`TokenBudgetTracker`, cut.** It duplicated `UsageLedger` (`cli/src/usage.ts`), which already enforces `NEXUS_CLOUD_MAX_TOKENS` with in-flight reservations.\n- **Integrated with PR #398.** The recovery context's \"Permitted next actions\" lists only actions the agent's role and mode allow (`renderRecoveryContext`'s `permits` predicate).\n- **Item 5 and the CERI and comparative harness entry points are not yet connected.** The throttle needs request latency from `cli/src/client.ts`. CERI needs a `nexus eval` command in `cli/src/index.ts`. Both files are claimed by other open work.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Workstream Roadmap Specifications\n\n### Item 1: Bounded Specialist Agent Profiles (Workstream A)\n- **Objective:** Introduce typed specialist profiles with strict tool permissions, input/output contracts, and token budget bounds.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** `ROLE_PROFILES` has 5 untyped text roles. No structured JSON schemas, no granular tool whitelisting beyond binary read-only mode.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Create `cli/src/core/specialist_profiles.ts`.\n  - Introduce `SpecialistContract`: permitted tools, input requirements, structured output schema, max context tokens, and escalation thresholds.\n  - Add specialist roles: `semantic_explorer`, `call_site_analyzer`, `failure_debugger`, `security_reviewer`, `acceptance_reviewer`.\n- **Dependencies:** `cli/src/core/roles.ts`, `cli/src/core/tool_groups.ts`.\n- **Priority:** High (P1) — foundational.\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Specialist units reject tools not in their declared profile with actionable errors.\n  - Specialist units emit structured, validated output artifacts.\n- **Verification Method:** Unit tests in `cli/test/specialist_profiles.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Purely additive; existing 5 roles remain backward-compatible defaults.\n- **Complexity:** Medium (3 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 2: Objective Independent Review Boundary (Workstream B)\n- **Objective:** Eliminate reviewer confirmation bias by constructing an objective evidence package stripped of the implementer's narrative claims, enforcing structured review findings.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** Reviewer units currently receive the implementer's summary narrative (`Completed prerequisite work: - <title>: <summary>`), introducing confirmation bias.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Create `cli/src/core/independent_review.ts`.\n  - Implement `buildReviewEvidencePackage`: extracts objective task requirements, authorized scope, base commit, unified diff, raw verifier receipts, and deliberately omits implementer turn narratives.\n  - Enforce structured review verdict schema:\n    ```ts\n    interface IndependentReviewVerdict {\n      verdict: \"ACCEPT\" | \"REJECT\";\n      verifiedObservations: string[];\n      suspectedDefects: Array<{ path: string; line?: number; description: string }>;\n      unprovenCriteria: string[];\n      scopeViolations: string[];\n    }\n    ```\n  - Disallow model-generated metrics from establishing authoritative `VERIFIED` status without deterministic verifier evidence.\n- **Dependencies:** Item 1 (`SpecialistContract`), `cli/src/core/independent_check.ts`.\n- **Priority:** Critical (P0) — addresses core verification integrity.\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Reviewer prompt contains zero sentences authored by the implementer.\n  - Reviewer output parse failure fails-closed as `REJECT`.\n  - Scope violations in diff are flagged deterministically.\n- **Verification Method:** Integration tests in `cli/test/independent_review.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Guarded by operation policy flag `policy.enforceIndependentReviewBoundary`.\n- **Complexity:** Medium (3 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 3: Deterministic Micro-Routing Engine (Workstream C) — cut from PR #396\n- **Objective:** Eliminate unnecessary LLM turns for non-semantic, deterministic commands and routine file/test operations.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** All in-turn tool calls are routed through the LLM. No classification for deterministic fast-paths.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Create `cli/src/core/micro_router.ts`.\n  - Implement rule-based intent matching for pure queries (test execution, symbol search, file reading).\n  - Constrain model-assisted routing with fallback to deterministic defaults.\n- **Dependencies:** None.\n- **Priority:** Medium (P2).\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Deterministic queries bypass inference turns with zero latency regression.\n  - Ambiguous queries fall back gracefully to model classification with full provenance logging.\n- **Verification Method:** Unit tests in `cli/test/micro_router.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Configurable via `policy.deterministicRouting: boolean`.\n- **Complexity:** Low-Medium (2 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 4: Multi-Stage Escalation State Machine (Workstream E) — cut from PR #396; superseded by PR #398 non-progress recovery\n- **Objective:** Replace naive retry loops with an observable escalation state machine that detects repeat failures, engages failure investigators, and escalates to deep reasoning models before blocking for operator input.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** Units retry naively up to `maxRunsPerUnit` on the same resource without diagnostic enrichment.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - State transitions: `INITIAL_ATTEMPT` -> `DIAGNOSTIC_ANALYSIS` -> `DEEP_REASONING_ESCALATION` -> `OPERATOR_DECISION`.\n  - Loop detection: identical error signatures trigger immediate escalation rather than burning budget.\n- **Dependencies:** Item 1 (Specialist Profiles).\n- **Priority:** Medium (P2).\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Repeated identical test failures trigger diagnostic specialist after 1 retry.\n  - Escalation respects model capability requirements and token bounds.\n- **Verification Method:** Unit tests in `cli/test/escalation_machine.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Additive state metadata; backward compatible with v2 schema.\n- **Complexity:** Medium (3 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 5: Telemetry-Informed Concurrency Throttle (Workstream F)\n- **Objective:** Dynamically adjust concurrent execution slots based on worker queue wait, TTFT, and latency telemetry.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** Static integer caps (`policy.maxConcurrentUnits`, `resource.maxConcurrentUnits`).\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Extend `ResourceRegistry` with rolling latency tracking.\n  - Throttle concurrency when latency spikes > 2x baseline, recovering on normalization.\n- **Dependencies:** `cli/src/core/resources.ts`.\n- **Priority:** Low-Medium (P3).\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Worker overload dynamically reduces active concurrency to prevent timeout cascades.\n- **Verification Method:** Simulation tests in `cli/test/adaptive_concurrency.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Can be disabled via `policy.adaptiveThrottle: false`.\n- **Complexity:** Medium (3 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 6: Comparative Evaluation Benchmark Harness (Workstream G)\n- **Objective:** Measure and contrast Configuration A (Baseline) vs Configuration B (Specialist + Independent Review) vs Configuration C (Adaptive).\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** Individual tests exist, but no unified comparative benchmark measuring token efficiency, defect escape rate, and confirmation bias.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Create `eval/comparative_orchestration_harness.ts` / `cli/src/eval/comparative_harness.ts`.\n  - Execute standard suites with telemetry recording.\n- **Dependencies:** Items 1, 2, 3.\n- **Priority:** Medium (P2).\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Produces structured comparative CSV/JSON metrics across identical benchmark tasks.\n- **Verification Method:** Benchmark dry-run on synthetic tasks.\n- **Complexity:** Medium (4 days).\n- **Implementation Status:** Complete in `cli/src/eval/comparative_harness.ts` (Commit `ad8ea909`).\n\n---\n\n### Item 7: Continuous Evaluation and Regression Intelligence (CERI)\n- **Objective:** Establish a continuous, evidence-based mechanism for detecting, investigating, classifying, and reporting degradation in Nexus's end-to-end engineering capabilities, enforcing that internal evaluations may veto releases but never certify correctness without independent validation.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** `eval/run.mjs` and `cli/src/evaluator.ts` execute tasks and capture candidate receipts, but lack versioned evaluation definitions, run manifests with full model/hardware provenance, designated baseline comparison, 12-state comparative classification, regression intelligence with epistemological distinction, and non-self-approving remediation pathways.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Workstream A: Versioned evaluation definitions and run manifests capturing immutable commit, dirty status, model identity, toolchain, and host environment.\n  - Workstream B: Append-only evidence persistence with atomic writes, secret redaction, and strict separation between tamper-evidence and independent validation receipts.\n  - Workstream C: Baseline comparator with eligibility validation and 12-state classification (`PASS`, `FAIL`, `REGRESSION_CANDIDATE`, `CONFIRMED_REGRESSION`, `IMPROVEMENT_CANDIDATE`, `FLAKY`, `INFRASTRUCTURE_FAILURE`, `EVALUATOR_DEFECT`, `INCONCLUSIVE`, `NOT_EXECUTED`, `CANCELLED`, `INCOMPARABLE`).\n  - Workstream D: Repeatability and flakiness analysis with bounded rerun policies.\n  - Workstream E: Capacity-aware scheduling for local dual-worker Intel Arc Pro environments.\n  - Workstream F: Representative end-to-end engineering evaluation benchmarks.\n  - Workstream G: Regression intelligence structuring findings into observation, inference, hypothesis, confirmed finding, and unresolved uncertainty.\n  - Workstream H: Reviewable remediation proposals with non-self-approval boundary.\n  - Workstream I: Structured machine-readable operational reporting with measurement limitations and blockers.\n- **Dependencies:** Items 1-6, `CandidateTracker`, `ValidationReceipt`, `isolate.mjs`.\n- **Priority:** High (P1) — Release qualification gate.\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Full provenance captured in run manifests without fabricating missing metadata.\n  - Incomparable baselines rejected explicitly rather than silently compared.\n  - Binary pass/fail forcing eliminated in favor of 12-state classification.\n  - Internal evaluations cannot mark candidates as verified without deterministic verifier receipts.\n  - Remediation proposals enforce `approvalStatus: \"PENDING_REVIEW\"`.\n- **Verification Method:** Unit and integration test suites in `cli/test/ceri_*.test.ts`.\n- **Operational Risks:** False regression alarms on non-comparable environments; benchmark overfitting; token consumption during reruns.\n- **Rollback Approach:** Purely additive modules in `cli/src/ceri/`; gated by config flag `ceri.enabled`.\n- **Complexity:** High (5 days).\n- **Implementation Status:** Foundational primitives and libraries implemented in PR #396 as standalone modules, covered by unit tests only and not yet used by the agent runtime (contracts, tamper-evident evidence store, baseline comparator, epistemological investigation, non-self-approving remediation, operational reporting, and comparative evaluation framework). Interactive agent loop integration and live dual-worker GPU scheduling are scheduled for subsequent integration PRs.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Deferred Scope (Targeted for v1.2)\n\nThe following items are explicitly **deferred** to release v1.2:\n1. **Hosted / Multi-Tenant Concurrency:** Nexus trust boundary remains local single-operator.\n2. **Autonomous Tool Generation:** Dynamic creation of new tool definitions by models without human oversight.\n3. **Automatic PR Publication (`git.publish`):** Requires operation-level egress and policy controls.\n4. **Interactive TUI Multi-Agent Pane:** Requires terminal PTY driver redesign.\n# Nexus Code v1.1.2 Implementation Plan: Specialist Orchestration & Independent Review\n\n**Document ID:** `NEXUS-PLAN-V112`  \n**Author:** Principal Software Architect & Senior Implementation Engineer  \n**Status:** Approved for Implementation  \n**Base Revision:** `origin/main` (`9a3d8787`)  \n\n---\n\n## 1. Architectural Strategy & Phasing\n\nTo maintain production stability and backward compatibility, the implementation proceeds in five ordered vertical slices:\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Slice 1: Specialist Agent Contracts & Profiles (Foundation) │\n│ - Typed SpecialistContract schema                           │\n│ - Tool permission whitelists per specialist                 │\n│ - Structured output envelope                                │\n└──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘\n                               │\n┌──────────────────────────────▼──────────────────────────────┐\n│ Slice 2: Objective Independent Review Boundary (Integrity)  │\n│ - buildReviewEvidencePackage (strips narrative)             │\n│ - Strict IndependentReviewVerdict schema                    │\n│ - Verification gating rules                                 │\n└──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘\n                               │\n┌──────────────────────────────▼──────────────────────────────┐\n│ Slice 3: Deterministic Micro-Routing Engine (Efficiency)    │\n│ - Rule-based fast-path matching for non-semantic queries    │\n│ - Constrained model-assisted routing fallback               │\n└──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘\n                               │\n┌──────────────────────────────▼──────────────────────────────┐\n│ Slice 4: Multi-Stage Escalation State Machine (Resilience)  │\n│ - Repeat failure detection & diagnostic routing             │\n│ - Deep reasoning escalation path (Qwen3.5-27B)              │\n└──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘\n                               │\n┌──────────────────────────────▼──────────────────────────────┐\n│ Slice 5: Telemetry-Informed Concurrency Throttle & Harness  │\n│ - Dynamic slot throttle under worker latency pressure       │\n│ - Comparative benchmark harness                             │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Slice 1: Specialist Agent Contracts & Profiles\n\n### 2.1 Modules & Files Changed\n- **New Module:** `cli/src/core/specialist_profiles.ts`\n- **Updated Module:** `cli/src/core/roles.ts` (exporting expanded roles and contracts)\n- **New Tests:** `cli/test/specialist_profiles.test.ts`\n\n### 2.2 Data Contracts & Interfaces\n```ts\nexport type SpecialistRole =\n  | \"semantic_explorer\"\n  | \"call_site_analyzer\"\n  | \"failure_debugger\"\n  | \"security_reviewer\"\n  | \"acceptance_reviewer\";\n\nexport interface SpecialistContract {\n  role: SpecialistRole;\n  title: string;\n  description: string;\n  permittedTools: readonly string[];\n  readOnly: boolean;\n  maxContextTokens: number;\n  inputRequirements: readonly string[];\n  outputSchemaName: string;\n}\n\nexport interface SpecialistStructuredOutput<T = unknown> {\n  specialistRole: SpecialistRole;\n  status: \"SUCCESS\" | \"FAILED\" | \"INCONCLUSIVE\";\n  findings: T;\n  evidence: Array<{ path: string; lineRange?: [number, number]; note: string }>;\n  tokenConsumption?: { prompt: number; completion: number };\n}\n```\n\n### 2.3 Invariants & Security\n- A specialist assigned to a unit cannot execute tools outside `permittedTools`. If a prohibited tool is called, the execution harness fails-closed with code `E_TOOL_DISALLOWED`.\n- Read-only specialists (`readOnly: true`) strictly enforce `--read-only` flag on the runner.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Slice 2: Objective Independent Review Boundary\n\n### 3.1 Modules & Files Changed\n- **New Module:** `cli/src/core/independent_review.ts`\n- **Updated Module:** `cli/src/core/operation_runner_execution.ts` (using objective evidence package when building reviewer prompts)\n- **New Tests:** `cli/test/independent_review.test.ts`\n\n### 3.2 Data Contracts & Interfaces\n```ts\nexport interface ReviewEvidencePackage {\n  operationId: string;\n  unitId: string;\n  objective: string;\n  authorizedScope: readonly string[];\n  baselineCommit: string;\n  candidateCommit?: string;\n  unifiedDiff: string;\n  verifierOutput?: string;\n  testPassRate?: { passed: number; failed: number; total: number };\n}\n\nexport interface IndependentReviewVerdict {\n  verdict: \"ACCEPT\" | \"REJECT\";\n  verifiedObservations: string[];\n  suspectedDefects: Array<{ path: string; line?: number; description: string }>;\n  unprovenCriteria: string[];\n  scopeViolations: string[];\n}\n```\n\n### 3.3 Confirmation Bias Mitigation\n- `buildReviewEvidencePackage`:\n  - Strips all free-form text narratives authored by the implementer.\n  - Injects only: (1) Objective requirements, (2) authorized file paths, (3) Git unified diff, (4) raw compiler/test runner stdout/stderr.\n  - Reviewer prompt states: `\"You are evaluating the raw code changes and test evidence independently. The implementer's self-assessed completion notes have been intentionally redacted.\"`\n\n---\n\n## 4. Slice 3: Deterministic Micro-Routing Engine (cut from PR #396)\n\n> Not delivered: nothing in the runtime routed on it, so `micro_router.ts` and its tests were removed. The design below is kept as history. See `ROADMAP.md`, \"Status in PR #396\".\n\n### 4.1 Modules & Files Changed\n- **New Module:** `cli/src/core/micro_router.ts`\n- **New Tests:** `cli/test/micro_router.test.ts`\n\n### 4.2 Interfaces\n```ts\nexport type RoutingDecisionKind = \"DETERMINISTIC_FASTPATH\" | \"SPECIALIST_DELEGATION\" | \"GENERAL_MODEL\";\n\nexport interface RoutingDecision {\n  kind: RoutingDecisionKind;\n  matchedRule?: string;\n  targetTool?: string;\n  targetRole?: string;\n  reason: string;\n  confidence: number;\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Persistence, State, & Migration Implications\n\n- **Schema Stability:** The operation persistence schema (`cli/src/core/operation.ts`) remains on **`schemaVersion: 2`**.\n- Specialist contracts and review packages operate cleanly within existing `OperationMessage` (kinds `\"artifact\"` and `\"verdict\"`) and `WorkUnit` models.\n- No schema migration required from v1.1.1 or v1.1.2-rc.1.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Verification Milestones\n\n1. **Milestone 1 (Specialist Profiles):** `specialist_profiles.test.ts` passes with 100% test coverage; tool whitelist rejections validated.\n2. **Milestone 2 (Review Boundary):** `independent_review.test.ts` passes; implementer narrative redaction verified; structured verdict parsing verified.\n3. **Milestone 3 (Micro-Router, cut from PR #396):** `micro_router.test.ts` passes; rule-based queries execute without LLM round-trips.\n4. **Milestone 4 (Full Regression):** Complete existing test suite (`npm --prefix cli test`) runs without regressions.\n## 2. Milestone v1.1.0: Observability Foundations & Eval Harness\n\n**Target:** Initial feature release post-v1.0.0\n**Theme:** Establish the local trace standard, sanitize flight recordings, and migrate evaluation from ad-hoc scripts to versioned datasets with reference oracles.\n\n### Work Packages\n\n| ID | Title | Summary & Deliverables | Backlog / Audit Source |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **WP-1101** | **OpenTelemetry Local Tracing** | Implement `cli/src/core/trace.ts` providing hierarchical tracing conforming to OpenTelemetry GenAI semantics. Captures agent turn roots, model request spans, tool execution spans, and context compactions into local `trace.jsonl` files. | `LANGSMITH_REVIEW` §2.1 |\n| **WP-1102** | **Flight Recorder Credential Redaction** | Implement automated zero-leak credential sanitization across all recorded spans, CLI error dumps, and debug journals, validating against the test fixtures in `docs/CRED003_FLIGHTRECORDER_SANITIZATION_EVIDENCE.md`. | `REQ-DX-105`, `CRED003` |\n| **WP-1103** | **Versioned Evaluation Datasets** | Migrate `eval/tasks/` into structured datasets under `eval/datasets/`. Decouple task input specifications (`examples.jsonl`) from evaluation assertions (`oracles.jsonl`). Add versioning and dataset splits (`regression`, `benchmark`). | `LANGSMITH_REVIEW` §2.3 |\n| **WP-1104** | **Standalone `nexus eval` Command** | Implement `nexus eval` subcommand in launcher grammar, supporting `--dataset <path>`, `--runs <count>`, `--isolated-home`, and structured JSON score outputs. | `REQ-EVL-101`, `ROADMAP.md` |\n| **WP-1105** | **Deterministic Code Evaluators** | Implement modular evaluator library in `eval/evaluators/code/`: AST syntax validation, build pass/fail, typecheck exit codes, diff boundary compliance, and JSON repair rates. | `LANGSMITH_REVIEW` §4.1 |\n| **WP-1106** | **Single Step Loop Mode** | Implement `--step` flag in `nexus run` and `/step` REPL command to execute one agent turn and pause deterministically for operator confirmation. | `REQ-AGT-111` |\n| **WP-1107** | **Constrained Decoding & GBNF Tool Grammar Engine** | Implement dynamic Context-Free Grammar (GBNF) generation from registered tool schemas (`read_file`, `write_file`, `edit_file`, `run_command`). Interfaces with `llama.cpp` (`grammar`), `vLLM` (`guided_json`), and `Ollama` (`format`). Supports hybrid grammars (`<think>...</think>` + tool call) for reasoning models (DeepSeek R1). Targets near-zero JSON argument parse errors and enables reliable agentic execution on 7B/14B Q4_K_M local models. | `POSITIONING.md` §3.6, `SPEC-EVALUATION.md` §6 |\n| **WP-1108** | **Zero Technical Debt & Dependency Modernization** | Triage, validate, and integrate the 27 post-v1.0.0 Dependabot PRs (#103–#129) under an absolute zero-deprecation policy for third-party libraries. Categorized across 5 staged batches: Core Rust runtime (#105, #107, #110, #113), GitHub Actions CI workflows (#103, #104, #106, #108, #112), Cloudflare micro-workers (#109, #115–#118, #121–#123, #125–#128), TypeScript compiler (#119, #124, #129), and CLI runtime migrations (#111, #114, #120 including Commander 12→15 compatibility audit). Enforces continuous zero-technical-debt invariants. | PRs #103–#129, `ROADMAP.md` §2.1 |\n\n### v1.1.0 Release Gates\n\n- **Gate G1.1-1 (Zero-Leak Tracing):** Local trace engine produces valid OTel GenAI spans for all turn steps without leaking environment credentials or writing outside `~/.local/share/nexus/traces/`.\n- **Gate G1.1-2 (Dataset Decoupling):** Evaluator executes 100% of benchmark tasks with zero leakage of `oracles.jsonl` to agent prompt context.\n- **Gate G1.1-3 (CLI Parity):** `nexus eval` executes cleanly from packaged binary installations without developer dependencies.\n- **Gate G1.1-4 (Constrained Decoding Reliability):** GBNF-constrained execution achieves >=99.5% valid JSON tool call syntax (minimizing syntax repair retries) across supported local inference endpoints (llama.cpp, vLLM, Ollama).\n- **Gate G1.1-5 (Zero Technical Debt & Zero Deprecated Packages):** Strictly 0 deprecated packages, 0 unmaintained dependencies, 0 CodeQL alerts, and 0 compiler warnings repository-wide. All 27 Dependabot PR upgrades pass unit/PTY suites, `cargo deny check`, automated npm registry deprecation scans, package reproducibility (`cmp`), and CLI option compatibility without breaking unprivileged sandbox invariants.\n\n### 2.1 Dependabot Triage & Integration Matrix (WP-1108)\n\nFollowing the PR #8 integration merge to `main`, Dependabot opened 27 automated dependency update pull requests (PRs #103–#129). These are triaged into 5 distinct integration batches scheduled for the v1.1.0 development cycle (deferred from v1.0.0):\n\n#### Batch 1: Core Rust Engine Runtime (Low Risk / Initial v1.1.0 Release Cycle)\nPatches to Rust core dependencies that preserve API compatibility and improve stability and performance:\n- **PR #105:** `tokio` (1.53.1 → 1.53.3 in `/core`) — Async runtime maintenance patch.\n- **PR #107:** `similar` (2.7.0 → 3.2.0 in `/core`) — Text diffing library major SemVer upgrade.\n- **PR #110:** `libc` (0.2.189 → 0.2.191 in `/core`) — System call bindings patch.\n- **PR #113:** `serde_json` (1.0.151 → 1.0.154 in `/core`) — Serialization optimization patch.\n- *Verification Requirement:* `cargo test --locked --manifest-path core/Cargo.toml` under isolated HOME; bit-for-bit packaging `cmp` check.\n\n#### Batch 2: Cloudflare Experimental Micro-Workers (Low Risk / Isolated)\nMaintenance updates for the isolated Cloudflare artifacts vertical slice (retired contest contract track):\n- **PRs #125, #126, #127, #128:** `vite` (8.3.3 → 8.3.4 across root and worker packages).\n- **PRs #115, #117, #118:** `@cloudflare/vite-plugin` (2.0.0-beta.sha-52b0dc0e9 → 2.0.0-beta.v.1).\n- **PRs #109, #116, #121, #122, #123:** `cf` (1.0.0-beta.10 → 1.0.0-beta.18 across worker bundles).\n- *Verification Requirement:* `npm run build --prefix experiments/cloudflare-artifacts-vertical-slice` and typecheck pass.\n\n#### Batch 3: GitHub Actions CI Workflows (Medium Risk / CI Pipeline Validation)\nModernization of hosted automation actions in `.github/workflows/`:\n- **PR #103:** `actions/attest-build-provenance` (commit hash update for SLSA build provenance).\n- **PR #104:** `actions/checkout` (v4.4.0 → v7.0.1) — Major runner checkout upgrade.\n- **PR #106:** `actions/download-artifact` (v4.3.0 → v8.0.2) — Artifact fetcher upgrade.\n- **PR #108:** `softprops/action-gh-release` (v2.6.2 → v3.0.3) — GitHub release asset upload upgrade.\n- **PR #112:** `actions/setup-node` (v4.4.0 → v7.1.0) — Node environment provisioning upgrade.\n- *Verification Requirement:* Staged workflow validation on test branch; assert identical artifact download and release asset publishing behavior.\n\n#### Batch 4: TypeScript Compiler & Typing Toolchain (Medium Risk / Type Safety Validation)\nModernization of the TypeScript compiler toolchain:\n- **PR #119:** `typescript` (5.9.3 → 7.0.2 in `/demo`) — Demo runner compiler bump.\n- **PR #124:** `@types/node` (22.20.4 → 26.6.5 in `/cli`) — Node.js runtime typings.\n- **PR #129:** `typescript` (5.9.3 → 7.0.2 in `/cli`) — Core CLI compiler bump.\n- *Verification Requirement:* Zero TypeScript compiler errors (`npm run build --prefix cli`, `tsc --noEmit`), clean declaration emission, and zero new `any` casts.\n\n#### Batch 5: Production CLI Runtime Libraries (High Risk / Breaking API Migration)\nMajor version bumps requiring focused API compatibility audits and code changes:\n- **PR #111:** `dotenv` (16.6.1 → 18.0.8 in `/cli`) — Environment loading library update. Verify untrusted workspace `.env` rejection invariants (REQ-SEC-001) remain strictly intact.\n- **PR #114:** `commander` (12.1.0 → 15.0.0 in `/cli`) — Major CLI option parser upgrade. Requires comprehensive audit of subcommands (`nexus run`, `check`, `models`, `upgrade`), option parsing (`--strict-token-count`, `--isolated-home`), and exit code propagation.\n- **PR #120:** `ora` (8.2.0 → 9.4.1 in `/cli`) — Terminal spinner library update. Validate stdout/stderr streaming, non-interactive CI fallback, and Ratatui TUI coexistence.\n- *Verification Requirement:* Full CLI unit and PTY suite (1,511+ tests), CLI journey tests (`cli/test/system_journey.test.ts`), and doctor verification.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Milestone v1.2.0: Experiment Comparison, Replay & Annotation\n\n**Target:** Second feature release post-v1.0.0\n**Theme:** Diagnostic reproducibility, statistical comparison across agent versions, interactive TUI trace inspection, and human annotation workflows.\n\n### Work Packages\n\n| ID | Title | Summary & Deliverables | Backlog / Audit Source |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **WP-1201** | **Deterministic Session Replay** | Implement `nexus replay <trace-id>`: Re-executes tool calls and container steps from a recorded trace inside an isolated bubblewrap container to reproduce bugs without re-calling the model. | `LANGSMITH_REVIEW` §1.1 |\n| **WP-1202** | **Experiment Comparator** | Implement `eval/compare.mjs` comparing two experiment runs with Wilson score 95% confidence intervals, token cost deltas, duration deltas, and regression matrices. | `LANGSMITH_REVIEW` §2.4 |\n| **WP-1203** | **Interactive TUI Trace Inspector** | Add a hierarchical trace inspector panel to `nexus-tui` (Ratatui), allowing operators to view the tool call tree, intermediate stdout, and latency distributions. | `LANGSMITH_REVIEW` §2.1 |\n| **WP-1204** | **Human Review & Annotation Queue** | Implement `nexus review <session-id>`: Interactive CLI / TUI prompt guiding reviewers through structured rubrics (tool correctness, intent fulfillment) and recording signed audit evidence. | `REQ-AGT-010`, `LANGSMITH_REVIEW` §6 |\n| **WP-1205** | **Heuristic Failure Clustering** | Implement automated classification of failing eval runs into structured failure modes (`TIMEOUT`, `LOOP_DETECTED`, `SCHEMA_CORRUPTION`, `UNAUTHORIZED_PATH`, `COMPILER_ERROR`). | `LANGSMITH_REVIEW` §7 |\n| **WP-1206** | **`/tdd` Phase Enforcement** | Enforce red-green-refactor phase invariants when running under test-driven development posture. | `VER-08`, `REQUIREMENTS.md` §5 |\n| **WP-1207** | **Composio Developer Ecosystem & Review Bridge** | Integrate Composio platform connectors (backed by Composio Startup Program Pro) to provide managed OAuth and bidirectional developer workflows: automated Linear and Jira issue creation from failure clusters (`WP-1205`), GitHub pull request review synchronization, and Slack/Discord notification dispatch for human annotation queues (`WP-1204`). Operates under strict zero-leak credential isolation (`CRED003`), unprivileged execution sandbox boundaries, and requires explicit operator confirmation for write actions. | Composio Startup Program, `POSITIONING.md` §3.8 |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Milestone v1.3.0: Standard OTLP Exporters & Platform Expansion\n\n**Target:** Enterprise & multi-platform release\n**Theme:** Standard OpenTelemetry network export, full context provenance graphs, knowledge lifecycle management, and macOS/arm64 architecture support.\n\n### Work Packages\n\n| ID | Title | Summary & Deliverables | Backlog / Audit Source |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **WP-1301** | **OpenTelemetry OTLP Exporter** | Add standard OTLP HTTP/gRPC exporter plug-in allowing operators to stream traces to local or network collectors (Jaeger, SigNoz, Langfuse, LangSmith) under strict egress allowlists. | `LANGSMITH_REVIEW` §9.3 |\n| **WP-1302** | **Full Context Provenance Graph** | Implement complete graph metadata for all context admissions (tracking origin file, trust classification, transformation history, and compaction survival). | `CTX-001`, `REQ-AGT-010` |\n| **WP-1303** | **macOS & arm64 Build Pipelines** | Add native GitHub Actions packaging runners and platform sandboxing (macOS `sandbox-exec` / Linux `arm64`). | `DEC-12`, `REQUIREMENTS.md` §5 |\n| **WP-1304** | **Knowledge Freshness & Lifecycle** | Implement automated staleness detection, contradiction resolution, and operator prompts for expiring project knowledge. | `CTX-002`, `REQUIREMENTS.md` §5 |\n| **WP-1305** | **cgroup v2 Fine-Grained Limits** | Add direct per-command CPU throttling and memory caps via systemd/cgroup-v2 scopes. | `REQUIREMENTS.md` §5 |\n| **WP-1306** | **Composio Enterprise Multi-App MCP Mesh** | Enable dynamic discovery and tool execution across 250+ enterprise SaaS applications (Salesforce, Google Workspace, AWS, Notion, Datadog, Sentry, Asana) using Composio's Model Context Protocol (MCP) toolsets. Compiles enterprise action schemas into logit-constrained GBNF grammars via `WP-1107` to enforce JSON schema syntactic compliance on local models. Enforces egress domain filtering, tmpfs execution isolation, and sovereign zero-telemetry defaults. | Composio Startup Program, `SPEC-OBSERVABILITY.md` §7 |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Architectural Invariants Across All Post-v1 Releases\n\n1. **Security & Boundary Preservation:** No post-v1 observability or evaluation feature may bypass or weaken the v1.0.0 security boundaries (untrusted `.env` rejection, SSRF blocking, container path confinement, or workspace trust).\n2. **Local Sovereignty:** Nexus remains 100% operational in air-gapped, offline, and self-hosted environments. External network transmission of telemetry is disabled by default and strictly opt-in.\n3. **Open Standards:** Telemetry formats conform to OpenTelemetry GenAI semantic conventions rather than proprietary vendor SDKs.\n4. **Reproducibility:** All benchmark datasets, evaluator code, and comparison tools are committed directly to the repository and execute deterministically under isolated `$HOME`.\n5. **Zero Technical Debt & Zero Deprecated Packages (Inviolable Invariant):**\n   - Strictly **NO deprecated packages or unmaintained third-party library dependencies** may ever be introduced, merged, or retained in any component or release—ever.\n   - Every npm dependency and Cargo crate must be on active, supported upstream releases.\n   - Automated CI gates fail closed on:\n     - Any npm package marked `deprecated` by the npm registry (`npm audit` and package-tree deprecation scans).\n     - Any Rust crate flagged as unmaintained, yanked, or deprecated in the RustSec advisory database (`cargo deny check bans advisories`).\n   - *Platform engines vs. libraries:* Platform runtime engine requirements (such as Node.js) follow explicit project architectural decisions (DEC-12), with upstream EOL status disclosed and modern LTS versions recommended.\n   - When upstream library dependencies announce deprecation or breaking lifecycles, Nexus must refactor, migrate, or replace them before minor release qualification. Under no circumstances may technical debt be deferred into permanent legacy layers.\n6. **Opt-In External Integrations & Boundary Invariant:**\n   - All third-party services and app connectors (including Composio developer bridges and enterprise app toolsets) are strictly **opt-in** and disabled by default.\n   - Nexus Code must always retain 100% operational capability in air-gapped, zero-network environments without external API keys.\n   - External tokens (`NEXUS_COMPOSIO_API_KEY`) must never be leaked into git commits, trace logs, or flight recordings (`CRED003`).\n   - High-impact or state-mutating actions on external services require explicit operator confirmation prompts before execution.\n# Nexus Code v1.1.2 Qualification Record\n\n**Status as of 2026-11-02: NOT QUALIFIED.** This record tracks the full WP-1101–WP-1108 scope selected for v1.1.2 and the five release gates in the [roadmap](../v1.1.0/ROADMAP.md). Evidence from a focused test, source review, earlier branch, or prior release is not a pass for the exact final candidate.\n\nThe v1.1.0 release failed and published no release files. v1.1.1 is the published predecessor. GitHub currently has no v1.1.2 release and the remote has no v1.1.2 tag. The v1.1.2 release notes and the former rc.1 draft describe the approved scope and the superseded narrow draft respectively.\n\n## Current v1.1.2 roadmap alignment\n\n`origin/main` at `a0d374e8` adds the repository's [Specialist Orchestration and Independent Review roadmap](../v1.1.2/ROADMAP.md). This record retains the user-approved WP-1101–WP-1108 scope above and tracks that current roadmap's remaining release commitments as well; neither scope is treated as a substitute for the other.\n\n| Roadmap item | Current source state | v1.1.2 qualification still required |\n|---|---|---|\n| Item 1 — Specialist contracts | Runtime roles, contracts, and tool permissions are connected in `origin/main`; PR #396 review fixes are present. | Run the exact-head specialist profile and runtime tests; review the integrated candidate. |\n| Item 2 — Independent review boundary | Reviewer evidence packaging and verdict gating are connected in `origin/main`. | Run exact-head boundary, verifier, and operation tests; independently review the integrated behavior. |\n| Item 3 — Deterministic micro-routing | Explicitly cut from PR #396; no runtime implementation is present in `origin/main`. The roadmap does not explicitly defer this item from the v1.1.2 release. | **OPEN.** Under the user-approved full-roadmap scope, implement and test deterministic query fast paths plus model fallback and provenance for ambiguous requests. |\n| Item 4 — Multi-stage escalation | Explicitly cut from PR #396 and superseded by merged PR #398 recovery behavior. | Qualify the superseding recovery behavior through the exact-head suite; do not reintroduce the cut state machine. |\n| Item 5 — Telemetry-informed throttle | Adaptive throttle code is present, but the roadmap says request-latency wiring and the CERI/CLI integration points remain unconnected. | Connect request latency to throttling, prove overload reduction and recovery, and test the runtime integration. |\n| Item 6 — Comparative evaluation harness | Comparative harness source is present in `origin/main`. | Verify its benchmark output and provenance on the integrated exact head. |\n| Item 7 — CERI | PR #396 supplies foundational modules, but the roadmap says they are not yet connected to the agent runtime and `nexus eval` entry point. | Complete the required CERI evaluation, provenance, classification, non-self-approval, and operational reporting integrations; meet the roadmap's release-qualification acceptance criteria. |\n\n## Work-package status\n\n| Package | Required outcome | Observed evidence | Status | Evidence still required for v1.1.2 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| WP-1101 — OpenTelemetry local tracing | Local GenAI spans for turns, model requests, tools, and compactions; credentials protected and trace output confined. | Prototype commit `8a83c9e7` lacks runtime event wiring and exact-head gate evidence. | **OPEN** | Integrated runtime wiring, tracing and redaction regressions, path-confinement proof, exact-head review and gates. |\n| WP-1102 — Flight recorder credential redaction | Zero-leak sanitization across traces, CLI errors, and debug journals. | Commit `ee7c8b1e` contains partial redaction work; operator acceptance remains pending. | **OPEN** | Full sink coverage, adversarial/credential fixtures, operator acceptance, and exact-head review. |\n| WP-1103 — Versioned evaluation datasets | Versioned datasets with separate examples/oracles and regression/benchmark splits; candidate prompts cannot access oracles. | Integration branch `v1/v112-wp1104-integration` is at docs-scope HEAD `eb6884df` with pending AC2 test and plan edits. The mount-view test passed focused 3/3; source-level peer review accepted the diff after removal of a duplicate, misleading evidence file. The full snapshot run at `00a8cb4d` failed (2,133 pass, 3 fail, 2 skipped; log SHA-256 `e6ea08b2b56c3f1ada337290db9ae0601921a23f99fd1ca50e7d95b507f870e9`) during concurrent PR396 test traffic. That snapshot is not the exact integration source and has no process-scope postcheck. | **PARTIAL / UNQUALIFIED** | Clean serialized full suite and process-scope postcheck on the exact integration source; resolve or independently classify all failures; complete fresh full-slice review and candidate-specific evidence. |\n| WP-1104 — Standalone `nexus eval` | Packaged command supports dataset selection, run count, isolated home, and structured score output. | Source commits `05c49df2` and `2a94f62e` were reviewed; `npm run build` passed. Focused six-task installed-candidate acceptance is recorded in `WP-1104-PLAN.md`, but it is not final integrated-candidate qualification. | **SOURCE SLICE CLOSED / RELEASE GATE OPEN** | Re-run applicable checks on the final integrated candidate; prove package parity and installed CLI operation without developer dependencies; fresh independent review. |\n| WP-1105 — Deterministic code evaluators | Modular AST, build, typecheck, diff-boundary, and JSON-repair evaluators. | Worktree `v1/wp-1105-evaluators` has untracked `eval/evaluators/` files and no WP-1105 commit or test evidence. Its documented dependency on WP-1103 remains open. | **OPEN** | Resolve owner handoff while preserving existing files; complete implementation, tests, review, and exact-head gate after WP-1103. |\n| WP-1106 — Single-step loop mode | `--step` and `/step` pause deterministically after one agent turn for operator confirmation. | An independently approved plan exists. Implementation requires `cli/src/agent.ts`, currently claimed by the PR396 agent. | **OPEN** | Obtain file handoff, implement and test the plan, then review and qualify on the integrated head. |\n| WP-1107 — GBNF tool grammar | Generate tool-schema grammars and integrate supported provider paths. | Prototype commit `9cb5f6de` is unintegrated; provider-path integration and validation are missing. | **OPEN** | Integrate providers and demonstrate the roadmap reliability target on supported endpoints; exact-head tests and review. |\n| WP-1108 — Dependency modernization | Triage and integrate the 27 Dependabot updates while meeting the zero-deprecation and compatibility requirements. | Branch `v1/wp-1108-dependency-modernization` is clean at `550112cd`, but has no PR or branch CI. Its triage record still lists integrated CodeQL/dependency scans, runtime compatibility, notice comparisons, package reproducibility, and release artifacts as outstanding. | **PARTIAL / UNQUALIFIED** | Owner/claim alignment; integrated scans and alerts; required test/PTY, `cargo deny`, npm deprecation, CLI compatibility, reproducibility, archive, checksum, SBOM, and installer evidence. |\n\n## Release-gate status\n\n| Gate | Required proof | Current evidence | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| G1.1-1 — Zero-leak tracing | Valid OTel GenAI spans for all required turn steps, no credential leakage, and writes confined to the approved trace directory. | WP-1101 prototype lacks runtime wiring and gate evidence. | **OPEN** |\n| G1.1-2 — Dataset decoupling | All benchmark tasks execute and no oracle data reaches candidate prompts; exact candidate and process-scope evidence retained. | Six-task focused acceptance and AC2 focused test exist; the latest full run failed on a non-exact snapshot under concurrent test traffic. | **PARTIAL / UNQUALIFIED** |\n| G1.1-3 — CLI parity | Packaged `nexus eval` works without developer dependencies on the exact release candidate. | Focused installed-candidate evidence exists; final integrated candidate is unverified. | **PARTIAL / UNQUALIFIED** |\n| G1.1-4 — Constrained decoding | At least 99.5% valid tool-call JSON across the supported llama.cpp, vLLM, and Ollama endpoints. | No qualifying cross-endpoint result found. | **OPEN** |\n| G1.1-5 — Zero technical debt | Zero deprecated/unmaintained packages, zero CodeQL alerts and compiler warnings, plus required suite/PTY, cargo-deny, npm scans, reproducibility, and CLI option compatibility without breaking unprivileged sandbox invariants. | WP-1108 branch has no branch CI; integrated scan and qualification evidence remain outstanding. | **OPEN** |\n\n## Exact-candidate release checklist\n\nThe final candidate remains unqualified until each applicable item is recorded against one exact commit and its artifacts:\n\n- All WP-1101–WP-1108 acceptance evidence and independent reviews are complete.\n- Current v1.1.2 roadmap items 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, and 7 are qualified on the exact candidate; item 4 is satisfied only if the superseding PR #398 recovery behavior passes exact-head qualification.\n- The full test suite, type checks, lint/security gates, migration and compatibility checks pass on the exact head.\n- Linux package outputs, checksums, SBOM, installer behavior, and reproducibility are verified for that head.\n- CLI option compatibility is verified without weakening or bypassing unprivileged sandbox invariants.\n- Installed-candidate acceptance covers evaluator isolation, oracle exclusion, provider behavior, and relevant operator scenarios.\n- Independent release acceptance approves the exact candidate and evidence packet.\n- The v1.1.2 tag and public release assets are published only after all prior gates pass.\n\nNo v1.1.2 release, tag, or publication is recorded by this document. Update the status and attach evidence as gates are completed; do not convert a partial or historical result into a release pass.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":737651540}},"started_at_ms":1793656377716,"completed_at_ms":1793656378455}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-02T21:52:59.665Z","ordinal":47729,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a19e40-5911-7330-8175-844bffe6cfec","call_id":"call_488d1dec12d648a2916c00415b7b08fa","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 3.3 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 12366)\nTotal output lines: 538\n\n# Nexus Code v1.1.2 Roadmap: Specialist Orchestration & Independent Review\n\n**Milestone:** Nexus Code `v1.1.2`  \n**Status:** In Progress  \n**Owner:** Principal Software Architect & Senior Implementation Engineer  \n**Document ID:** `NEXUS-ROADMAP-V112`  \n**Base Release:** `v1.1.1` (`b9dd0bcc`) / `v1.1.2-rc.1` (`c3e26103`)  \n\n---\n\n## 1. Roadmap Overview & Commitments\n\nThe v1.1.2 release line encompasses two complementary tiers:\n1. **Tier 1 (Completed in v1.1.2-rc.1):** Operational defect fix for multi-message prompt deduplication upon crash-resume ([PR #377](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/377)) and release documentation addendum.\n2. **Tier 2 (v1.1.2 Feature Enhancement):** Bounded specialist agent contracts, objective independent review boundaries (eliminating implementer confirmation bias), deterministic micro-routing (cut from PR #396), and telemetry-aware scheduling.\n\n### Status in PR #396 (owner decision: connect the new modules or cut them)\n- **Item 1, connected.** The specialist contracts are runtime roles in `cli/src/core/roles.ts`. An operation unit or `/team` target can run as one. The agent offers only the contract's permitted tools, and it refuses any other call when the call is dispatched (`roleToolAllowList`). That holds whether or not the role is read-only.\n- **Item 2, connected.** Units with the `reviewer` or `acceptance_reviewer` role run behind the boundary:\n  - Their prompt is the evidence package: the candidate diff against the operation's base commit, and the verifier receipt of the unit they depend on. Prerequisite summaries are withheld.\n  - The unit completes only on a well-formed `ACCEPT` that survives the deterministic gates. Prose, a malformed verdict, a reported defect or a failed verifier blocks the unit for an operator decision.\n  - The verifier receipt comes from the dependency's integration receipt (isolated operations) or, where nothing was integrated (for example `/team`), from the verification recorded on the dependency's completed result. A verification that is not a passing run that executed tests reaches the package as a non-zero exit code. A large (truncated) diff with no passing verifier evidence is rejected.\n  - Retrying a rejected review resumes the reviewer's conversation. A review unit or read-only specialist (`reviewer`, `acceptance_reviewer`, `semantic_explorer`, `call_site_analyzer`, `security_reviewer`; read-only by construction) does not inherit the write-verification obligation that recovery adds for writer units, since it can neither have written nor run a verifier; writer units, `failure_debugger` (which can run commands) and the read-only `researcher` keep the fail-closed obligation.\n  - The base a review diffs against is the isolated operation's base commit or, for a non-isolated operation in a Git checkout, the HEAD recorded when the operation first ran (`startCommit`), so a change a unit committed is still in the diff. The base is recorded only before any unit has started; an operation persisted without one, whose work already ran, is never given a base afterwards, and its review blocks. A persisted base is resolved with `git rev-parse --verify --end-of-options` before use.\n  - `security_reviewer` is deliberately not behind the boundary: only `reviewer` and `acceptance_reviewer` are. It is a read-only specialist whose contract lists `unified_diff` as an input, and it keeps the ordinary prompt.\n  - An empty candidate diff is treated as a failure to collect evidence (there is nothing to judge), and the worst verification across all dependencies decides the verifier exit code.\n  - If the candidate diff cannot be collected (no Git checkout, or the diff exceeds the 4 MiB buffer), the review is blocked before any model turn. That failure is never presented to the reviewer as evidence.\n  - The runtime package names the real operation id and declares no file scope: no operation or unit declares one, so the scope gate (`detectScopeViolations`) applies only to a package that supplies `authorizedScope`.\n  - The boundary complements `cli/src/core/independent_check.ts`, which decides whether a verifier pass is independent evidence. It does not replace it.\n- **Items 3 and 4, cut.** Nothing in the runtime routed on the micro-router. The escalation machine duplicated the non-progress recovery in PR #398, which is connected.\n- **`TokenBudgetTracker`, cut.** It duplicated `UsageLedger` (`cli/src/usage.ts`), which already enforces `NEXUS_CLOUD_MAX_TOKENS` with in-flight reservations.\n- **Integrated with PR #398.** The recovery context's \"Permitted next actions\" lists only actions the agent's role and mode allow (`renderRecoveryContext`'s `permits` predicate).\n- **Item 5 and the CERI and comparative harness entry points are not yet connected.** The throttle needs request latency from `cli/src/client.ts`. CERI needs a `nexus eval` command in `cli/src/index.ts`. Both files are claimed by other open work.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Workstream Roadmap Specifications\n\n### Item 1: Bounded Specialist Agent Profiles (Workstream A)\n- **Objective:** Introduce typed specialist profiles with strict tool permissions, input/output contracts, and token budget bounds.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** `ROLE_PROFILES` has 5 untyped text roles. No structured JSON schemas, no granular tool whitelisting beyond binary read-only mode.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Create `cli/src/core/specialist_profiles.ts`.\n  - Introduce `SpecialistContract`: permitted tools, input requirements, structured output schema, max context tokens, and escalation thresholds.\n  - Add specialist roles: `semantic_explorer`, `call_site_analyzer`, `failure_debugger`, `security_reviewer`, `acceptance_reviewer`.\n- **Dependencies:** `cli/src/core/roles.ts`, `cli/src/core/tool_groups.ts`.\n- **Priority:** High (P1) — foundational.\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Specialist units reject tools not in their declared profile with actionable errors.\n  - Specialist units emit structured, validated output artifacts.\n- **Verification Method:** Unit tests in `cli/test/specialist_profiles.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Purely additive; existing 5 roles remain backward-compatible defaults.\n- **Complexity:** Medium (3 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 2: Objective Independent Review Boundary (Workstream B)\n- **Objective:** Eliminate reviewer confirmation bias by constructing an objective evidence package stripped of the implementer's narrative claims, enforcing structured review findings.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** Reviewer units currently receive the implementer's summary narrative (`Completed prerequisite work: - <title>: <summary>`), introducing confirmation bias.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Create `cli/src/core/independent_review.ts`.\n  - Implement `buildReviewEvidencePackage`: extracts objective task requirements, authorized scope, base commit, unified diff, raw verifier receipts, and deliberately omits implementer turn narratives.\n  - Enforce structured review verdict schema:\n    ```ts\n    interface IndependentReviewVerdict {\n      verdict: \"ACCEPT\" | \"REJECT\";\n      verifiedObservations: string[];\n      suspectedDefects: Array<{ path: string; line?: number; description: string }>;\n      unprovenCriteria: string[];\n      scopeViolations: string[];\n    }\n    ```\n  - Disallow model-generated metrics from establishing authoritative `VERIFIED` status without deterministic verifier evidence.\n- **Dependencies:** Item 1 (`SpecialistContract`), `cli/src/core/independent_check.ts`.\n- **Priority:** Critical (P0) — addresses core verification integrity.\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Reviewer prompt contains zero sentences authored by the implementer.\n  - Reviewer output parse failure fails-closed as `REJECT`.\n  - Scope violations in diff are flagged deterministically.\n- **Verification Method:** Integration tests in `cli/test/independent_review.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Guarded by operation policy flag `policy.enforceIndependentReviewBoundary`.\n- **Complexity:** Medium (3 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 3: Deterministic Micro-Routing Engine (Workstream C) — cut from PR #396\n- **Objective:** Eliminate unnecessary LLM turns for non-semantic, deterministic commands and routine file/test operations.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** All in-turn tool calls are routed through the LLM. No classification for deterministic fast-paths.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Create `cli/src/core/micro_router.ts`.\n  - Implement rule-based intent matching for pure queries (test execution, symbol search, file reading).\n  - Constrain model-assisted routing with fallback to deterministic defaults.\n- **Dependencies:** None.\n- **Priority:** Medium (P2).\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Deterministic queries bypass inference turns with zero latency regression.\n  - Ambiguous queries fall back gracefully to model classification with full provenance logging.\n- **Verification Method:** Unit tests in `cli/test/micro_router.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Configurable via `policy.deterministicRouting: boolean`.\n- **Complexity:** Low-Medium (2 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 4: Multi-Stage Escalation State Machine (Workstream E) — cut from PR #396; superseded by PR #398 non-progress recovery\n- **Objective:** Replace naive retry loops with an observable escalation state machine that detects repeat failures, engages failure investigators, and escalates to deep reasoning models before blocking for operator input.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** Units retry naively up to `maxRunsPerUnit` on the same resource without diagnostic enrichment.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - State transitions: `INITIAL_ATTEMPT` -> `DIAGNOSTIC_ANALYSIS` -> `DEEP_REASONING_ESCALATION` -> `OPERATOR_DECISION`.\n  - Loop detection: identical error signatures trigger immediate escalation rather than burning budget.\n- **Dependencies:** Item 1 (Specialist Profiles).\n- **Priority:** Medium (P2).\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Repeated identical test failures trigger diagnostic specialist after 1 retry.\n  - Escalation respects model capability requirements and token bounds.\n- **Verification Method:** Unit tests in `cli/test/escalation_machine.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Additive state metadata; backward compatible with v2 schema.\n- **Complexity:** Medium (3 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 5: Telemetry-Informed Concurrency Throttle (Workstream F)\n- **Objective:** Dynamically ad…7166 tokens truncated…p connectors (including Composio developer bridges and enterprise app toolsets) are strictly **opt-in** and disabled by default.\n   - Nexus Code must always retain 100% operational capability in air-gapped, zero-network environments without external API keys.\n   - External tokens (`NEXUS_COMPOSIO_API_KEY`) must never be leaked into git commits, trace logs, or flight recordings (`CRED003`).\n   - High-impact or state-mutating actions on external services require explicit operator confirmation prompts before execution.\n# Nexus Code v1.1.2 Qualification Record\n\n**Status as of 2026-11-02: NOT QUALIFIED.** This record tracks the full WP-1101–WP-1108 scope selected for v1.1.2 and the five release gates in the [roadmap](../v1.1.0/ROADMAP.md). Evidence from a focused test, source review, earlier branch, or prior release is not a pass for the exact final candidate.\n\nThe v1.1.0 release failed and published no release files. v1.1.1 is the published predecessor. GitHub currently has no v1.1.2 release and the remote has no v1.1.2 tag. The v1.1.2 release notes and the former rc.1 draft describe the approved scope and the superseded narrow draft respectively.\n\n## Current v1.1.2 roadmap alignment\n\n`origin/main` at `a0d374e8` adds the repository's [Specialist Orchestration and Independent Review roadmap](../v1.1.2/ROADMAP.md). This record retains the user-approved WP-1101–WP-1108 scope above and tracks that current roadmap's remaining release commitments as well; neither scope is treated as a substitute for the other.\n\n| Roadmap item | Current source state | v1.1.2 qualification still required |\n|---|---|---|\n| Item 1 — Specialist contracts | Runtime roles, contracts, and tool permissions are connected in `origin/main`; PR #396 review fixes are present. | Run the exact-head specialist profile and runtime tests; review the integrated candidate. |\n| Item 2 — Independent review boundary | Reviewer evidence packaging and verdict gating are connected in `origin/main`. | Run exact-head boundary, verifier, and operation tests; independently review the integrated behavior. |\n| Item 3 — Deterministic micro-routing | Explicitly cut from PR #396; no runtime implementation is present in `origin/main`. The roadmap does not explicitly defer this item from the v1.1.2 release. | **OPEN.** Under the user-approved full-roadmap scope, implement and test deterministic query fast paths plus model fallback and provenance for ambiguous requests. |\n| Item 4 — Multi-stage escalation | Explicitly cut from PR #396 and superseded by merged PR #398 recovery behavior. | Qualify the superseding recovery behavior through the exact-head suite; do not reintroduce the cut state machine. |\n| Item 5 — Telemetry-informed throttle | Adaptive throttle code is present, but the roadmap says request-latency wiring and the CERI/CLI integration points remain unconnected. | Connect request latency to throttling, prove overload reduction and recovery, and test the runtime integration. |\n| Item 6 — Comparative evaluation harness | Comparative harness source is present in `origin/main`. | Verify its benchmark output and provenance on the integrated exact head. |\n| Item 7 — CERI | PR #396 supplies foundational modules, but the roadmap says they are not yet connected to the agent runtime and `nexus eval` entry point. | Complete the required CERI evaluation, provenance, classification, non-self-approval, and operational reporting integrations; meet the roadmap's release-qualification acceptance criteria. |\n\n## Work-package status\n\n| Package | Required outcome | Observed evidence | Status | Evidence still required for v1.1.2 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| WP-1101 — OpenTelemetry local tracing | Local GenAI spans for turns, model requests, tools, and compactions; credentials protected and trace output confined. | Prototype commit `8a83c9e7` lacks runtime event wiring and exact-head gate evidence. | **OPEN** | Integrated runtime wiring, tracing and redaction regressions, path-confinement proof, exact-head review and gates. |\n| WP-1102 — Flight recorder credential redaction | Zero-leak sanitization across traces, CLI errors, and debug journals. | Commit `ee7c8b1e` contains partial redaction work; operator acceptance remains pending. | **OPEN** | Full sink coverage, adversarial/credential fixtures, operator acceptance, and exact-head review. |\n| WP-1103 — Versioned evaluation datasets | Versioned datasets with separate examples/oracles and regression/benchmark splits; candidate prompts cannot access oracles. | Integration branch `v1/v112-wp1104-integration` is at docs-scope HEAD `eb6884df` with pending AC2 test and plan edits. The mount-view test passed focused 3/3; source-level peer review accepted the diff after removal of a duplicate, misleading evidence file. The full snapshot run at `00a8cb4d` failed (2,133 pass, 3 fail, 2 skipped; log SHA-256 `e6ea08b2b56c3f1ada337290db9ae0601921a23f99fd1ca50e7d95b507f870e9`) during concurrent PR396 test traffic. That snapshot is not the exact integration source and has no process-scope postcheck. | **PARTIAL / UNQUALIFIED** | Clean serialized full suite and process-scope postcheck on the exact integration source; resolve or independently classify all failures; complete fresh full-slice review and candidate-specific evidence. |\n| WP-1104 — Standalone `nexus eval` | Packaged command supports dataset selection, run count, isolated home, and structured score output. | Source commits `05c49df2` and `2a94f62e` were reviewed; `npm run build` passed. Focused six-task installed-candidate acceptance is recorded in `WP-1104-PLAN.md`, but it is not final integrated-candidate qualification. | **SOURCE SLICE CLOSED / RELEASE GATE OPEN** | Re-run applicable checks on the final integrated candidate; prove package parity and installed CLI operation without developer dependencies; fresh independent review. |\n| WP-1105 — Deterministic code evaluators | Modular AST, build, typecheck, diff-boundary, and JSON-repair evaluators. | Worktree `v1/wp-1105-evaluators` has untracked `eval/evaluators/` files and no WP-1105 commit or test evidence. Its documented dependency on WP-1103 remains open. | **OPEN** | Resolve owner handoff while preserving existing files; complete implementation, tests, review, and exact-head gate after WP-1103. |\n| WP-1106 — Single-step loop mode | `--step` and `/step` pause deterministically after one agent turn for operator confirmation. | An independently approved plan exists. Implementation requires `cli/src/agent.ts`, currently claimed by the PR396 agent. | **OPEN** | Obtain file handoff, implement and test the plan, then review and qualify on the integrated head. |\n| WP-1107 — GBNF tool grammar | Generate tool-schema grammars and integrate supported provider paths. | Prototype commit `9cb5f6de` is unintegrated; provider-path integration and validation are missing. | **OPEN** | Integrate providers and demonstrate the roadmap reliability target on supported endpoints; exact-head tests and review. |\n| WP-1108 — Dependency modernization | Triage and integrate the 27 Dependabot updates while meeting the zero-deprecation and compatibility requirements. | Branch `v1/wp-1108-dependency-modernization` is clean at `550112cd`, but has no PR or branch CI. Its triage record still lists integrated CodeQL/dependency scans, runtime compatibility, notice comparisons, package reproducibility, and release artifacts as outstanding. | **PARTIAL / UNQUALIFIED** | Owner/claim alignment; integrated scans and alerts; required test/PTY, `cargo deny`, npm deprecation, CLI compatibility, reproducibility, archive, checksum, SBOM, and installer evidence. |\n\n## Release-gate status\n\n| Gate | Required proof | Current evidence | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| G1.1-1 — Zero-leak tracing | Valid OTel GenAI spans for all required turn steps, no credential leakage, and writes confined to the approved trace directory. | WP-1101 prototype lacks runtime wiring and gate evidence. | **OPEN** |\n| G1.1-2 — Dataset decoupling | All benchmark tasks execute and no oracle data reaches candidate prompts; exact candidate and process-scope evidence retained. | Six-task focused acceptance and AC2 focused test exist; the latest full run failed on a non-exact snapshot under concurrent test traffic. | **PARTIAL / UNQUALIFIED** |\n| G1.1-3 — CLI parity | Packaged `nexus eval` works without developer dependencies on the exact release candidate. | Focused installed-candidate evidence exists; final integrated candidate is unverified. | **PARTIAL / UNQUALIFIED** |\n| G1.1-4 — Constrained decoding | At least 99.5% valid tool-call JSON across the supported llama.cpp, vLLM, and Ollama endpoints. | No qualifying cross-endpoint result found. | **OPEN** |\n| G1.1-5 — Zero technical debt | Zero deprecated/unmaintained packages, zero CodeQL alerts and compiler warnings, plus required suite/PTY, cargo-deny, npm scans, reproducibility, and CLI option compatibility without breaking unprivileged sandbox invariants. | WP-1108 branch has no branch CI; integrated scan and qualification evidence remain outstanding. | **OPEN** |\n\n## Exact-candidate release checklist\n\nThe final candidate remains unqualified until each applicable item is recorded against one exact commit and its artifacts:\n\n- All WP-1101–WP-1108 acceptance evidence and independent reviews are complete.\n- Current v1.1.2 roadmap items 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, and 7 are qualified on the exact candidate; item 4 is satisfied only if the superseding PR #398 recovery behavior passes exact-head qualification.\n- The full test suite, type checks, lint/security gates, migration and compatibility checks pass on the exact head.\n- Linux package outputs, checksums, SBOM, installer behavior, and reproducibility are verified for that head.\n- CLI option compatibility is verified without weakening or bypassing unprivileged sandbox invariants.\n- Installed-candidate acceptance covers evaluator isolation, oracle exclusion, provider behavior, and relevant operator scenarios.\n- Independent release acceptance approves the exact candidate and evidence packet.\n- The v1.1.2 tag and public release assets are published only after all prior gates pass.\n\nNo v1.1.2 release, tag, or publication is recorded by this document. Update the status and attach evidence as gates are completed; do not convert a partial or historical result into a release pass.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a19de1-2f43-7663-a2d9-f17adf8cb101","create_time":1793656379.6656942}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
